{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788282182138,"sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9731a92bf970a436","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6 && venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport mcp\nprint('mcp version:', mcp.__version__ if hasattr(mcp, '__version__') else 'n/a')\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nprint('FastMCP OK')\nimport inspect\nprint([m for m in dir(FastMCP) if not m.startswith('_')][:20])\n\""},"output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 4, in <module>\n  File \"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mcp/server/fastmcp.py\", line 16, in <module>\n    raise ModuleNotFoundError(_MESSAGE, name=__name__)\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'mcp.server.fastmcp'. This is mcp 2.x, where FastMCP was renamed to MCPServer (from mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer) and other APIs changed; see the migration guide at https://py.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/migration/#fastmcp-renamed-to-mcpserver or pin 'mcp<2' to keep running v1 code.\nmcp version: n/a\n","metadata":{"output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 4, in <module>\n  File \"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mcp/server/fastmcp.py\", line 16, in <module>\n    raise ModuleNotFoundError(_MESSAGE, name=__name__)\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'mcp.server.fastmcp'. This is mcp 2.x, where FastMCP was renamed to MCPServer (from mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer) and other APIs changed; see the migration guide at https://py.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/migration/#fastmcp-renamed-to-mcpserver or pin 'mcp<2' to keep running v1 code.\nmcp version: n/a\n","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6 && venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport mcp\nprint('mcp version:', mcp.__version__ if hasattr(mcp, '__version__') else 'n/a')\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nprint('FastMCP OK')\nimport inspect\nprint([m for m in dir(FastMCP) if not m.startswith('_')][:20])\n\"","time":{"start":1788282182093,"end":1788282182137}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the data landscape. Team names vary a lot:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\", \"Palmeiras\", \"América - MG\", \"Atletico Mineiro\", \"Atlético-MG\", \"EC Bahia\", \"Bahia\", \"Sao Paulo-SP\", \"São Paulo\"\n- FIFA clubs: \"Santos\", \"Santos Laguna\", \"Flamengo\", \"Palmeiras\" — wait, the sample only found Santos clubs with those filters. Let me check for Flamengo in FIFA.\n\nAlso note the FIFA data is FIFA 19 or so (Messi at Barcelona age 31 → FIFA 19). So the clubs are circa 2018-19. Question says \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" — Flamengo should be in the data.\n\nTeam name normalization is key. Let me design:\n\n**Normalization strategy**:\n1. Strip accents (NFKD → ASCII)\n2. Lowercase\n3. Remove common suffixes/prefixes: state suffixes (\"-SP\", \" - RJ\"), country markers (\"(URU)\", \"-EQU\"), club form words (\"Sport Club\", \"Esporte Clube\", \"FC\", \"EC\", ...)\n4. Remove punctuation/duplicate spaces\n\nFor matching a query team like \"Corinthians\" against dataset names like \"Corinthians-SP\", \"Corinthians\", \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\":\n- normalize both → \"corinthians\" vs \"corinthians\" — strip \"-SP\" suffix → matches.\n\nBut careful with names like \"Sport-PE\" → normalized \"sport\" and \"Sport Recife\" → \"sport recife\". Hmm, \"Sport\" is a real team (Sport Club do Recife). \"Sport Recife\" in BR-Football would normalize to \"sport recife\" which won't match \"sport\". For matching, I'll use a canonical-name registry with alias resolution: exact normalized match first, then substring/startswith matching with preference.\n\nActually, for the level of this task, a reasonable approach:\n- `normalize_team(name)`: lowercase, strip accents, remove parenthetical country markers, remove state suffix patterns (2-letter UF at end), remove club legal words, remove punctuation → canonical key like \"corinthians\", \"sao paulo\", \"atletico mineiro\".\n- Build a registry mapping canonical key → display name (most common raw form) and set of variants.\n\nMatching for queries: normalize the query the same way; exact match on key, else fuzzy (startswith / contains / difflib).\n\nNow let me design the whole deliverable. The task: \"Create an MCP server that provides a knowledge graph interface for Brazilian soccer data\" connected to an LLM. The MCP server must expose tools covering:\n\n1. Match queries: by team, date range, competition, season; head-to-head\n2. Team queries: stats (W/D/L, GF/GA), by competition/season, home/away splits\n3. Player queries: search by name, nationality, club, ratings\n4. Competition queries: standings by season, finals/brackets, champions, relegated teams\n5. Statistical analysis: avg goals, home vs away, biggest wins, best records\n\nArchitecture:\n- `server.py` (or package `brazilian_soccer/`): MCP server (stdio) built with `mcp` python SDK (FastMCP). \n- `data_loader.py`: loads all 6 CSVs into normalized dataclasses; team name normalization.\n- `queries.py` / `analytics.py`: the query functions.\n- `tests/`: pytest BDD-style tests (given/when/then named test functions), covering the gherkin scenarios.\n\nThe mcp SDK 2.1.1 — let me check its API. The modern SDK: `from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP`. In 2.x, it might be `from mcp.server import Server` or the new `mcp.server.fastmcp` still exists. Let me check what's importable.\n\nAlso for the \"knowledge graph interface\" phrase — the spec says \"provides a knowledge graph interface\" loosely; the actual requirement is tools that let an LLM query players/teams/matches/competitions. I'll expose MCP tools. I could also build an actual in-memory graph (entities/relations) but the core need is query tools. I'll keep it focused: MCP tools as the interface.\n\nLet me check the mcp SDK API surface quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dec92f3001aXtt5C152ur1xQ","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","messageID":"msg_05dec2edf0014TFoCR9TDDx1Zb"}}
