{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788282386360,"sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ba8e956da5cbd1b0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6 && venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport mcp.client as c\nprint([m for m in dir(c) if not m.startswith('_')])\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import StdioServerParameters, stdio_client\nimport inspect\nprint(inspect.signature(stdio_client))\nfrom mcp import ClientSession\nprint('ClientSession OK', [m for m in dir(ClientSession) if not m.startswith('_')][:25])\n\""},"output":"['CacheConfig', 'CacheEntry', 'CacheKey', 'CacheMode', 'ClaimContext', 'Client', 'ClientExtension', 'ClientRequestContext', 'ClientSession', 'InMemoryResponseCacheStore', 'IncomingMessage', 'InputRequiredRoundsExceededError', 'NotificationBinding', 'ResponseCacheStore', 'ResultClaim', 'Transport', 'UnexpectedClaimedResult', 'advertise', 'caching', 'client', 'context', 'extension', 'session', 'session_group', 'sse', 'stdio', 'streamable_http', 'subscriptions']\n(server: mcp.client.stdio.StdioServerParameters, errlog: <class 'TextIO'> = <_io.TextIOWrapper name='<stderr>' mode='w' encoding='utf-8'>) -> collections.abc.AsyncGenerator[tuple[mcp.shared._stream_protocols.ReadStream[mcp.shared.message.SessionMessage | Exception], mcp.shared._stream_protocols.WriteStream[mcp.shared.message.SessionMessage]], None]\nClientSession OK ['adopt', 'call_tool', 'complete', 'discover', 'discover_result', 'dispatch_input_request', 'get_prompt', 'initialize', 'initialize_result', 'instructions', 'list_prompts', 'list_resource_templates', 'list_resources', 'list_tools', 'protocol_version', 'read_resource', 'send_discover', 'send_notification', 'send_ping', 'send_progress_notification', 'send_request', 'send_roots_list_changed', 'server_capabilities', 'server_info', 'set_logging_level']\n","metadata":{"output":"['CacheConfig', 'CacheEntry', 'CacheKey', 'CacheMode', 'ClaimContext', 'Client', 'ClientExtension', 'ClientRequestContext', 'ClientSession', 'InMemoryResponseCacheStore', 'IncomingMessage', 'InputRequiredRoundsExceededError', 'NotificationBinding', 'ResponseCacheStore', 'ResultClaim', 'Transport', 'UnexpectedClaimedResult', 'advertise', 'caching', 'client', 'context', 'extension', 'session', 'session_group', 'sse', 'stdio', 'streamable_http', 'subscriptions']\n(server: mcp.client.stdio.StdioServerParameters, errlog: <class 'TextIO'> = <_io.TextIOWrapper name='<stderr>' mode='w' encoding='utf-8'>) -> collections.abc.AsyncGenerator[tuple[mcp.shared._stream_protocols.ReadStream[mcp.shared.message.SessionMessage | Exception], mcp.shared._stream_protocols.WriteStream[mcp.shared.message.SessionMessage]], None]\nClientSession OK ['adopt', 'call_tool', 'complete', 'discover', 'discover_result', 'dispatch_input_request', 'get_prompt', 'initialize', 'initialize_result', 'instructions', 'list_prompts', 'list_resource_templates', 'list_resources', 'list_tools', 'protocol_version', 'read_resource', 'send_discover', 'send_notification', 'send_ping', 'send_progress_notification', 'send_request', 'send_roots_list_changed', 'server_capabilities', 'server_info', 'set_logging_level']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6 && venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport mcp.client as c\nprint([m for m in dir(c) if not m.startswith('_')])\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import StdioServerParameters, stdio_client\nimport inspect\nprint(inspect.signature(stdio_client))\nfrom mcp import ClientSession\nprint('ClientSession OK', [m for m in dir(ClientSession) if not m.startswith('_')][:25])\n\"","time":{"start":1788282386315,"end":1788282386359}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I see the full complexity. 163 colliding bases. The right design:\n\n**Canonical team registry**: map every raw name → canonical team ID. Strategy:\n\n1. Normalize: strip accents, lowercase, remove parens content EXCEPT country markers for foreign teams (e.g. \"Nacional (URU)\" vs \"Nacional (PAR)\" vs \"Nacional - AM\" — three different teams!), strip trailing UF for Brazilian clubs, keep country code for foreign clubs.\n\nThe UF-stripping is correct for most Brazilian clubs (the state suffix is redundant when the base name is distinctive: \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras\"). The problem cases are where multiple clubs share the same base+UF-stripped name:\n- \"atletico\" (MG, GO, PR) — need UF retained as disambiguator\n- \"america\" (MG, RN)\n- \"botafogo\" (RJ, PB, SP)\n- \"sport\"... only PE is common in these datasets\n- \"nacional\" (AM vs foreign URU/PAR)\n- \"santos\" (SP vs AP vs Santos Laguna etc.)\n- \"fluminense\" (RJ vs PI)\n- \"santa cruz\" (PE vs RN vs RS)\n- \"guarani\" (SP vs CE vs PAR)\n- etc.\n\nThe key insight: **the state suffix matters when the base alone is ambiguous**. So the canonical key should be `base + uf` when UF is present, i.e., \"atletico mg\", \"atletico go\", \"atletico pr\". And for names without UF like \"Atletico Mineiro\" (BR-Football), map to \"atletico mg\" via a curated alias table.\n\nSo the plan:\n- Canonical key = normalized base + UF when the raw name carries a UF suffix. E.g. \"Atlético-MG\" → key \"atletico-mg\"; \"Palmeiras-SP\" → key \"palmeiras-sp\"... hmm but then \"Palmeiras\" (Libertadores file, no suffix) → key \"palmeiras\" ≠ \"palmeiras-sp\". \n\nSo I need alias unification: a curated table that says \"palmeiras-sp\", \"palmeiras\" → same team; \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico mineiro\", \"atletico\" (only when MG implied) → same.\n\nRather than agonize over auto-magic, curate: collect all distinct raw names across the 5 match files (I have the collision list; total distinct raw names is maybe ~700), and build a mapping where:\n1. Auto-generate candidate key: norm(base + uf).\n2. Curated OVERRIDES for known equivalences (BR-Football names without UF → UF-ful keys; \"Athletico-PR\" = \"Atlético-PR\" = \"Athletico Paranaense\"; \"Atletico Mineiro\" = \"Atlético-MG\"; etc.)\n\nThen for query matching: user provides e.g. \"Flamengo\". Normalize → \"flamengo\". Look up alias table: \"flamengo\" → canonical \"flamengo-rj\" (most prominent). Also \"Flamengo - PI\" is a tiny club from Copa do Brasil; the alias table maps bare \"flamengo\" to the most prominent club (RJ). \n\nFor robust user matching, implement a scoring match: exact canonical match > alias match > unique base match > substring match with disambiguation (if multiple, pick the one with most matches in data, and report alternatives).\n\nThis is the pragmatic approach used by real projects. The curated table needs to cover:\n1. The BR-Football names (no UF): \"Sao Paulo\" → \"sao paulo-sp\"... wait actually BR-Football \"Sao Paulo\" corresponds to São Paulo FC. Fine: \"sao paulo\" → sao-paulo-sp key via alias.\n2. FIFA club names: \"Grêmio\" → gremio; \"Atlético Mineiro\" → atletico-mg; \"Sport Club do Recife\" → sport-pe; \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → america-mg; \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → ceara-ce; \"Atlético Paranaense\" → athletico-pr; \"Chapecoense\" → chapecoense-sc; \"Paraná\" → parana-pr; \"Vitória\" → vitoria-ba; \"Bahia\" → bahia-ba; \"Botafogo\" → botafogo-rj; \"Fluminense\" → fluminense-rj; \"Cruzeiro\" → cruzeiro-mg; \"Internacional\" → internacional-rs; \"Santos\" → santos-sp.\n\nSince FIFA only has ~14 Brazilian clubs, that's easy.\n\nDecision on canonical key format: I'll use a canonical ID like \"flamengo-rj\"? Or keep UF in the ID only for ambiguous bases? Consistency: always include UF when known for Brazilian clubs — \"palmeiras-sp\", \"flamengo-rj\" — and alias the bare forms to them. That's deterministic and matches the primary dataset convention (Brasileirao_Matches.csv uses \"Palmeiras-SP\").\n\nForeign clubs (Libertadores): \"Boca Juniors\", \"River Plate\", \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Olimpia-PAR\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Peñarol\". Keep country in key for ambiguous foreign names: \"nacional-uru\", \"nacional-par\"; plain otherwise (\"boca juniors\", \"river plate\"). The Libertadores file itself uses \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Olimpia-PAR\" conventions.\n\nMatching flow for a query team name:\n1. normalize_query → key form\n2. exact match against canonical registry (which includes all raw names from files + curated aliases) → done\n3. base match: strip UF from query, find all canonical teams whose base matches → if exactly one, use it; if multiple, choose most prominent (match count) and note alternatives\n4. substring/startswith match (e.g., \"Corinthians Paulista\" → \"corinthians-sp\"): find canonicals containing query base as substring or vice versa → pick best by difflib score; note alternatives.\n\nAlso handle full official names: \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" — normalized \"sport club corinthians paulista\" contains \"corinthians\". Substring matching handles it. FIFA names like \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" — parens stripped → \"america fc minas gerais\" hmm, contains \"minas gerais\" — curated alias: \"america fc (minas gerais)\" → america-mg. OK.\n\nNow the amount of curated alias work is bounded. Let me now design the code structure.\n\n### Package layout\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer/\n  __init__.py          # exports SoccerData, load_soccer_data\n  models.py            # Match, Player, TeamStats dataclasses\n  normalize.py         # normalize_team_name, TeamRegistry (build from data + curated aliases)\n  loader.py            # SoccerData.load() — all 6 CSVs, unified match store w/ dedup\n  queries.py           # query functions (pure, testable)\nserver.py              # MCP server (stdio) — thin tool wrappers over queries\ntests/\n  conftest.py\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_matches.py\n  test_teams.py\n  test_players.py\n  test_competitions.py\n  test_statistics.py\n  test_server.py       # end-to-end MCP protocol via subprocess\nrequirements.txt\n```\n\n### Match model\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Match:\n    date: date | None\n    competition: str          # canonical competition name\n    season: int | None\n    stage: str | None         # round/stage\n    home_team: str            # canonical id\n    away_team: str\n    home_goals: int | None\n    away_goals: int | None\n    venue: str | None\n    source: str              # file name\n```\n\nCompetitions (canonical):\n- \"Brasileirão Série A\" — from Brasileirao_Matches.csv + novo (2003-2011; dedup 2012-2019) + BR-Football Serie A (2023; dedup overlaps)\n- \"Copa do Brasil\" — Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv + BR-Football Copa do Brasil (dedup)\n- \"Copa Libertadores\" — Libertadores_Matches.csv\n- \"Brasileirão Série B\" — BR-Football Serie B\n- \"Brasileirão Série C\" — BR-Football Serie C\n\nDedup natural key: (date, home_id, away_id, home_goals, away_goals, competition). Same match appears in multiple files with possibly minor differences — e.g. same match in Brasileirao_Matches and BR-Football (both have Serie A 2014-2022). Dedup keeps first occurrence by source priority: official competition files first (Brasileirao_Matches, Brazilian_Cup_Matches, Libertadores_Matches), then novo, then BR-Football.\n\nWait — but novo is ALSO Brasileirão Série A 2003-2019. Overlap with Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2019. Same match (same date/teams/score) → dedup handles it. Are the dates identical across files? novo uses DD/MM/YYYY; Brasileirao uses \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\". Same calendar date presumably. Scores should match. Let me sanity check the dedup count after loading.\n\nAlso note: novo has teams like \"Athletico-PR\" while Brasileirao_Matches has \"Atlético-PR\" for the same club — my registry must map both to the same canonical ID (athletico-pr). And \"Grêmio Prudente\" is separate from \"Grêmio\".\n\nEdge cases in data:\n- Libertadores: 1 row with season 'NA' and missing goals (\"Flamengo - - Athletico\") — the 2022 final! Let me look at it: season NA, datetime NA, teams Flamengo vs Athletico, goals missing. It's the 2022 Libertadores final ( Flamengo 1-0 Athletico ). The file has 'final' stage rows for 2020 (Palmeiras 1-0 Santos). The NA row is garbage-ish but shouldn't crash. I'll keep it with season=None, date=None, goals=None and make all analytics tolerant to None goals/date.\n- BR-Football: goals are floats \"1.0\"; corners etc. floats.\n- fifa_data.csv has BOM + unnamed first column.\n\n### Query functions (tools)\n\nMCP tools (the LLM-facing API):\n\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=20)` → list of matches + total count\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None)` → matches between two teams + W/D/L summary per team\n3. `last_meeting(team_a, team_b)` → most recent match (spec: \"When did Flamengo last play Corinthians?\")\n4. `team_record(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=\"all\")` → W/D/L, GF/GA, matches; venue: all/home/away\n5. `team_profile(team)` → overview: competitions played, seasons, matches, W/D/L, GF/GA, best wins... plus players at that club (cross-file query: player + match data)\n6. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_rating=None, max_rating=None, order_by=\"overall\", limit=20)` → players\n7. `player_profile(name)` → full attributes\n8. `top_players(nationality=None, club=None, position=None, limit=10)` → highest rated\n9. `standings(competition, season)` → computed table (top N + full), points, W/D/L, GF/GA\n10. `champion(competition, season)` → winner of league season or cup final\n11. `competition_finals(competition, season=None)` → final matches of cup competitions (Copa do Brasil last round, Libertadores 'final' stage)\n12. `relegated(competition, season, n=4)` → bottom n teams\n13. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, team=None, limit=10)` → largest goal margins\n14. `league_averages(competition=None, season=None)` → avg goals/match, home win%, away win%, draw%\n15. `best_records(competition=None, season=None, venue=\"all\", metric=\"win_rate\", limit=10)` → which team has best home/away record\n16. `top_scorers_by_club`... not inferable (no scorer data) — skip; \"if inferable\" per spec. Skip it.\n17. `derbies(season=None)` → matches between famous rival pairs (curated list of Brazilian derbies: Fla-Flu, Choque-Rei, Majestoso, Clássico Vovô, Gre-Nal, Atletiba, Ba-Vi, Clássico dos Milhões...). The spec asks \"Show me all derbies in 2023\" — find matches where teams are traditional rivals.\n18. `list_competitions()` → competitions + seasons available\n19. `search_teams(query)` → find canonical team names matching a query (helps LLM disambiguate)\n\nThat's a rich toolset. Each returns structured dict/JSON. \n\nNow, the \"knowledge graph interface\" phrase — the server connects an LLM to the data; MCP tools ARE the interface. I'll note in README.\n\n### MCP server implementation with mcp 2.x\n\nLet me check how the MCPServer 2.x tool decorator and stdio transport work — particularly the exact JSON-RPC framing on stdio (newline-delimited JSON or LSP-style Content-Length headers?). Let me write a quick spike: minimal server + subprocess client test.\n\nAlso, mcp 2.x has `mcp.client` too — I can use the client SDK for the e2e test! `mcp.client.stdio` presumably. Let me check available client modules. That would make the protocol test robust without hand-rolling framing.\n\nLet me spike the server now. First check `mcp.client` API.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05defb29f00117M8BChzJF8f1p","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","messageID":"msg_05dee2adf0015DoCvIXAlQQHt8"}}
