{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788282595232,"sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-af43d6475f7b35a2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6 && venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nfor r in rows:\n    if 'barbosa' in r['Name'].lower() or r['Name'] in ('Neymar Jr','Casemiro','Alisson','Gabriel Jesus'):\n        print(r['Name'], '|', r['Overall'], r['Position'], r['Club'], r['Age'], r['Nationality'])\nprint('---brazilians sorted---')\nbr = sorted([r for r in rows if r['Nationality']=='Brazil'], key=lambda r: -int(r['Overall']))\nfor r in br[:8]: print(r['Name'], r['Overall'], r['Position'], r['Club'])\n\""},"output":"Neymar Jr | 92 LW Paris Saint-Germain 26 Brazil\nCasemiro | 88 CDM Real Madrid 26 Brazil\nAlisson | 85 GK Liverpool 25 Brazil\nGabriel Jesus | 83 ST Manchester City 21 Brazil\nM. Barbosa | 76 GK Villarreal CF 33 Argentina\nHélder Barbosa | 71 LM Akhisar Belediyespor 31 Portugal\nBarbosaldinho | 70 LCB Chapecoense 30 Brazil\n---brazilians sorted---\nNeymar Jr 92 LW Paris Saint-Germain\nCasemiro 88 CDM Real Madrid\nCoutinho 88 LW FC Barcelona\nMarcelo 88 LB Real Madrid\nThiago Silva 88 RCB Paris Saint-Germain\nEderson 86 GK Manchester City\nRoberto Firmino 86 CAM Liverpool\nAlex Sandro 86 LB Juventus\n","metadata":{"output":"Neymar Jr | 92 LW Paris Saint-Germain 26 Brazil\nCasemiro | 88 CDM Real Madrid 26 Brazil\nAlisson | 85 GK Liverpool 25 Brazil\nGabriel Jesus | 83 ST Manchester City 21 Brazil\nM. Barbosa | 76 GK Villarreal CF 33 Argentina\nHélder Barbosa | 71 LM Akhisar Belediyespor 31 Portugal\nBarbosaldinho | 70 LCB Chapecoense 30 Brazil\n---brazilians sorted---\nNeymar Jr 92 LW Paris Saint-Germain\nCasemiro 88 CDM Real Madrid\nCoutinho 88 LW FC Barcelona\nMarcelo 88 LB Real Madrid\nThiago Silva 88 RCB Paris Saint-Germain\nEderson 86 GK Manchester City\nRoberto Firmino 86 CAM Liverpool\nAlex Sandro 86 LB Juventus\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6 && venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nfor r in rows:\n    if 'barbosa' in r['Name'].lower() or r['Name'] in ('Neymar Jr','Casemiro','Alisson','Gabriel Jesus'):\n        print(r['Name'], '|', r['Overall'], r['Position'], r['Club'], r['Age'], r['Nationality'])\nprint('---brazilians sorted---')\nbr = sorted([r for r in rows if r['Nationality']=='Brazil'], key=lambda r: -int(r['Overall']))\nfor r in br[:8]: print(r['Name'], r['Overall'], r['Position'], r['Club'])\n\"","time":{"start":1788282595203,"end":1788282595226}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The MCP 2.x client works. Small API notes:\n- `res.structured_content` (snake_case), not `structuredContent`\n- The `add` tool returned fine; only my attribute name was wrong.\n\nThe spike succeeded: `tools: ['add']`, and `call_tool` worked (the AttributeError was after the call, on reading the result).\n\nNow let me build the real implementation. Plan:\n\n**Files:**\n1. `brazilian_soccer/__init__.py` — public API\n2. `brazilian_soccer/models.py` — dataclasses\n3. `brazilian_soccer/normalize.py` — team name normalization + registry + curated aliases\n4. `brazilian_soccer/loader.py` — CSV loading, unified Match store with dedup\n5. `brazilian_soccer/queries.py` — all query/analytics functions\n6. `server.py` — MCP server entry point\n7. `tests/conftest.py` + test modules (BDD style)\n8. `requirements.txt`\n9. `pytest.ini` or pyproject? Keep simple: `pytest.ini` with testpaths.\n\nLet me think carefully about the normalize module design.\n\n```python\nBRAZILIAN_UFS = {26 states}\n\ndef _strip_accents(s) -> str: NFKD → ascii\ndef _basic_clean(s) -> str: lowercase, strip accents, remove parentheticals, remove 'antigo', replace '-' with ' ', collapse spaces\ndef _split_uf(tokens) -> (base_tokens, uf or None): if last token in UFS → strip it\n```\n\nCanonicalization: raw name → canonical_id.\n\nFor a raw name from data:\n1. clean → tokens\n2. Detect country marker: parenthetical like \"(URU)\", \"(PAR)\", \"(EQU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Olimpia-PAR\" — Libertadores suffixes like \"-EQU\", \"-PAR\". Handle: last token in {URU, PAR, EQU, ARG, CHI, COL, PER, BOL, VEN, MEX, USA?} → foreign country marker.\n3. If trailing UF (Brazilian state) → key = base + \"-\" + uf\n4. If no trailing UF → key = base (may be ambiguous, resolved via alias table)\n\nBut wait: \"Nacional - AM\" (Amazonas Brazilian club) vs \"Nacional (URU)\" vs \"Nacional (PAR)\": AM is a Brazilian UF → \"nacional-am\"; URU → foreign \"nacional-uru\"; PAR (Paraguay) — but \"PAR\" is not a Brazilian UF, it's Paraguay country marker → \"nacional-par\". \n\nBrazilian UFs: AC, AL, AP, AM, BA, CE, DF, ES, GO, MA, MT, MS, MG, PA, PB, PR, PE, PI, RJ, RN, RS, RO, RR, SC, SP, SE, TO.\nForeign country codes seen in Libertadores: URU, PAR, EQU, ARG, CHI, COL, PER, BOL, VEN, MEX. Let me check what actually appears.\n\nThen the alias/merge table: canonical key → merged group. E.g.:\n- \"atletico-pr\" and \"athletico-pr\" → both exist! Brasileirao file uses \"Atlético-PR\"; novo uses \"Athletico-PR\". Merge to one: canonical display \"Athletico-PR\" (current official name) — I'll pick canonical id \"athletico-pr\" and alias \"atletico-pr\" → \"athletico-pr\".\n- BR-Football \"Athletico Paranaense\" → \"athletico paranaense\" → alias to \"athletico-pr\".\n- \"Atletico Mineiro\" → \"atletico-mg\". \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\" → \"atletico mineiro mg\" → hmm. \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\": clean → \"atletico mineiro mg\" → trailing UF \"mg\" → base \"atletico mineiro\", key \"atletico mineiro-mg\"?? That's ugly.\n\nRefine: key format = base + uf joined with \"-\"? \"atletico mineiro-mg\" vs \"atletico-mg\" differ. I need the alias table to unify: \"atletico mineiro-mg\" ≡ \"atletico-mg\". So after generating the raw key, look through a curated EQUIVALENCE list of regex-ish mappings.\n\nAlternative cleaner approach: canonical key = base + \"-\" + uf where base itself is normalized; then maintain MERGE rules as (pattern → canonical) pairs. Let me define canonical teams as a curated dict covering the prominent clubs:\n\n```python\nCANONICAL_TEAMS = {\n  \"flamengo-rj\": \"Flamengo\",\n  \"fluminense-rj\": \"Fluminense\",\n  \"palmeiras-sp\": \"Palmeiras\",\n  \"corinthians-sp\": \"Corinthians\",\n  \"sao paulo-sp\": \"São Paulo\",\n  \"santos-sp\": \"Santos\",\n  \"gremio-rs\": \"Grêmio\",\n  \"internacional-rs\": \"Internacional\",\n  \"botafogo-rj\": \"Botafogo\",\n  \"vasco da gama-rj\": \"Vasco da Gama\",\n  \"atletico-mg\": \"Atlético Mineiro\",\n  \"athletico-pr\": \"Athletico Paranaense\",\n  \"atletico-go\": \"Atlético Goianiense\",\n  \"cruzeiro-mg\": \"Cruzeiro\",\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, but then \"Botafogo-PB\" and \"Botafogo-SP\" need separate entries \"botafogo-pb\", \"botafogo-sp\". And the raw-name→key algorithm produces exactly \"botafogo-pb\" etc. So the algorithm covers most; the curated part handles only the no-suffix variants and merges.\n\nLet me simplify: the registry is built dynamically from the data:\n1. For each raw name in all match files, compute key via algorithm (keeps UF).\n2. Build `key → [raw variants]` map with match counts.\n3. Apply curated MERGE pairs to unify across files (e.g. \"atletico mineiro-mg\" + \"atletico-mg\" + \"atletico mineiro\"; \"athletico-pr\" + \"atletico-pr\" + \"athletico paranaense\" + \"atletico paranaense\"; \"am�ria fc minas gerais\" + \"america-mg\"...).\n4. For query resolution: normalize the query (no UF usually) → if query key exactly matches a registry key OR exactly matches a base of exactly one key → resolve; if base ambiguous → pick most-prominent and report alternatives; else fuzzy substring via difflib.\n\nFor the merge step, I'll write a curated list of (base_without_uf_variants) tuples. Actually here's a cleaner formulation. For BR-Football names without UF, the name itself often contains the state hint or a distinctive full name. The curated alias map (query-form → canonical key) handles the prominent ones; for obscure clubs without suffix, base-key matching may occasionally be ambiguous but those clubs appear in only one file, so a \"same base\" match within that file is unambiguous enough.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider: is a fully-curated approach for ALL teams feasible? The distinct base names across files number maybe 300-400 Brazilian clubs (Copa do Brasil has MANY small clubs). Curating 400 entries by hand is overkill. The algorithmic approach + small curated merge list (~40 entries for prominent clubs) + prominence-based disambiguation is right.\n\nProminence = total match appearances across all files. \"flamengo\" bare query → candidates: \"flamengo-rj\" (hundreds of matches), \"flamengo-pi\" (a couple). Pick \"flamengo-rj\", mention alternatives. \n\nAlso handle the special case: base with NO UF variant that is itself in the registry — e.g. Libertadores file has \"Palmeiras\" (no -SP). The algorithm gives key \"palmeiras\". Then MERGE: \"palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras-sp\" via curated merge (since \"palmeiras-sp\" exists and \"palmeiras\" base matches exactly). Rule: **if a key without UF has the same base as exactly one UF-ful key, merge into the UF-ful key**. Wait but that breaks foreign teams? \"palmeiras\" base unique among UF keys? \"santos\" base → \"santos-sp\" and \"santos-ap\" — two candidates, ambiguous, can't auto-merge! For those, curated merge: \"santos\" → \"santos-sp\" (prominence-based). OK so the rule becomes: auto-merge when unique, else curated-or-prominence. Hmm, but prominence auto-merge could be wrong (\"santos\" in Libertadores 2011+ file = Santos FC of SP, correct!). For Brazilian data 99% the prominent