mcp 2.x `@mcp.tool()`, if the function returns a dict, it's exposed as structuredContent. Returning strings gives text content. I'll return dicts (structured) — LLMs consume structure well. The spec's \"answer format\" examples are LLM-formulated; the tools provide the data.\n\nAlright — performance: 42K rows total; all in-memory Python lists; queries are linear scans — milliseconds. Fine.\n\nEncoding: open with utf-8-sig. Confirmed BOM in fifa (﻿ shown in head).\n\nNow the server entry. `server.py` at repo root:\n\n```python\nfrom brazilian_soccer import load_soccer_data\nfrom brazilian_soccer import queries\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\n\nmcp = MCPServer(name=\"brazilian-soccer-mcp\", ...)\ndata = load_soccer_data()  # module level, ~1-2s\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_matches(...): return queries.search_matches(data, ...)\n...\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    asyncio.run(mcp.run_stdio_async())\n```\n\nTests import `server` module? Importing server.py triggers data load at import (1-2s, fine) but NOT running stdio. For e2e test, spawn subprocess `python server.py` via mcp client StdioServerParameters. The client session: initialize → list_tools → call_tool. \n\nWait — one issue: the e2e test runs server.py as subprocess; the venv python must be used; in tests I can use `sys.executable` (pytest runs under venv). ✓\n\nAlso worth handling SIGTERM gracefully — stdio transport handles it.\n\nDate parsing: multiple formats:\n- \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" / \"2023-09-24\" ISO\n- \"29/03/2003\" Brazilian\n- Copa: \"2012-03-07 16:00:00\" same ISO+time\n- Libertadores \"NA\" → None\n- BR-Football \"2023-09-24\" ISO date, separate time column\n- fifa \"Joined\" dates not needed.\n\nparse_date(s): try formats [\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\", \"%Y/%m/%d\"?]. Also \"2012-03-07 16:00:00\" ✓.\n\nGoals parsing: int when possible; Libertadores goals are quoted strings \"2\"; BR-Football floats \"1.0\"; novo ints; some may be empty/NA → None. Use a to_int