{"type":"text","timestamp":1788282747508,"sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","part":{"id":"prt_05df4e312001ey3ds2ILseXI7w","messageID":"msg_05df3e090001sLTHWOecp21855","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","type":"text","text":"I caught design issues while writing (key format must use dashes consistently, merge redirects needed for player clubs). Let me rewrite `normalize.py` correctly.","time":{"start":1788282725138,"end":1788282747506}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788282747520,"sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b2afc4374004e75f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"Team name normalisation and the canonical team registry.\n\nThe datasets name the same club in many shapes:\n\n* with a state suffix:        ``Palmeiras-SP``, ``América - MG``\n* without a suffix:           ``Palmeiras``, ``América``\n* full/official names:        ``Sport Club Corinthians Paulista``\n* accented vs plain:          ``Grêmio`` vs ``Gremio``\n* foreign clubs with country: ``Nacional (URU)``, ``Olimpia-PAR``\n\nThis module turns every raw spelling into a canonical team id such as\n``\"palmeiras-sp\"`` or ``\"nacional-uru\"`` and provides a registry that can\nresolve user queries (\"Flamengo\", \"atletico\", \"Athletico Paranaense\") to a\ncanonical team, disambiguating by prominence when the base name is shared\nby several clubs (``Atlético-MG`` vs ``Atlético-GO`` vs ``Atlético-PR``).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport difflib\nimport re\nimport unicodedata\nfrom collections import Counter\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Constants\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n\n#: Brazilian state (UF) abbreviations used as team-name suffixes.\nBRAZILIAN_UFS = frozenset(\n    {\n        \"ac\", \"al\", \"ap\", \"am\", \"ba\", \"ce\", \"df\", \"es\", \"go\", \"ma\", \"mt\",\n        \"ms\", \"mg\", \"pa\", \"pb\", \"pr\", \"pe\", \"pi\", \"rj\", \"rn\", \"rs\", \"ro\",\n        \"rr\", \"sc\", \"sp\", \"se\", \"to\",\n    }\n)\n\n#: Country markers used in the Libertadores dataset, e.g. \"Nacional (URU)\".\nFOREIGN_MARKERS = frozenset(\n    {\n        \"uru\", \"par\", \"equ\", \"arg\", \"chi\", \"col\", \"per\", \"bol\", \"ven\",\n        \"mex\", \"usa\", \"jpn\", \"kor\", \"crc\", \"gtm\", \"hon\", \"slv\", \"pan\",\n        \"can\", \"dom\",\n    }\n)\n\n#: Filler tokens dropped from names (\"Boavista Sport Club (antigo ...)\").\n_FILLER = frozenset({\"antigo\"})\n\n#: Club legal-form tokens stripped from the ends of names (\"EC Bahia\" -> \"Bahia\").\n_FORM_TOKENS = frozenset({\"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"ac\", \"cbf\"})\n\n#: Curated key merges: keys produced by :func:`team_key` that refer to the\n#: same club under a structurally different spelling.  Most cross-file\n#: unification is automatic (bare name + state-suffixed twin); this table\n#: handles full names such as \"Atletico Mineiro\" == \"Atlético-MG\" and the\n#: historical \"Atlético-PR\" == \"Athletico-PR\" spellings.\nALIASES: dict[str, str] = {\n    \"atletico-mineiro\": \"atletico-mg\",\n    \"atletico-mineiro-mg\": \"atletico-mg\",\n    \"atletico-paranaense\": \"athletico-pr\",\n    \"atletico-paranaense-pr\": \"athletico-pr\",\n    \"atletico-pr\": \"athletico-pr\",\n    \"athletico-paranaense\": \"athletico-pr\",\n    \"athletico-paranaense-pr\": \"athletico-pr\",\n    \"atletico-goianiense\": \"atletico-go\",\n    \"atletico-goianiense-go\": \"atletico-go\",\n    \"atletico-goianiense-go-go\": \"atletico-go\",\n    \"sport-recife\": \"sport-pe\",\n    \"sport-recife-pe\": \"sport-pe\",\n    \"sport-club-do-recife\": \"sport-pe\",\n    \"nautico-capibaribe\": \"nautico-pe\",\n    \"nautico-capibaribe-pe\": \"nautico-pe\",\n    \"nautico\": \"nautico-pe\",\n    \"ceara-sporting-club\": \"ceara-ce\",\n    \"ceara-sporting-club-ce\": \"ceara-ce\",\n    \"america-fc-minas-gerais\": \"america-mg\",\n    \"corinthians-paulista\": \"corinthians-sp\",\n    \"sport-club-corinthians-paulista\": \"corinthians-sp\",\n    \"rb-bragantino\": \"red-bull-bragantino-sp\",\n    \"operario-ferroviario-esporte-c-pr\": \"operario-pr\",\n    \"operario-ferroviario\": \"operario-pr\",\n}\n\n#: Display names for prominent clubs (accents restored; the state suffix is\n#: kept only where the bare base name would be ambiguous).\n_DISPLAY_OVERRIDES = {\n    \"flamengo-rj\": \"Flamengo\",\n    \"fluminense-rj\": \"Fluminense\",\n    \"vasco-da-gama-rj\": \"Vasco da Gama\",\n    \"botafogo-rj\": \"Botafogo\",\n    \"palmeiras-sp\": \"Palmeiras\",\n    \"corinthians-sp\": \"Corinthians\",\n    \"sao-paulo-sp\": \"São Paulo\",\n    \"santos-sp\": \"Santos\",\n    \"portuguesa-sp\": \"Portuguesa\",\n    \"ponte-preta-sp\": \"Ponte Preta\",\n    \"red-bull-bragantino-sp\": \"Red Bull Bragantino\",\n    \"guarani-sp\": \"Guarani\",\n    \"santo-andre-sp\": \"Santo André\",\n    \"sao-caetano-sp\": \"São Caetano\",\n    \"gremio-rs\": \"Grêmio\",\n    \"internacional-rs\": \"Internacional\",\n    \"juventude-rs\": \"Juventude\",\n    \"caxias-rs\": \"Caxias\",\n    \"atletico-mg\": \"Atlético-MG\",\n    \"america-mg\": \"América-MG\",\n    \"cruzeiro-mg\": \"Cruzeiro\",\n    \"athletico-pr\": \"Athletico-PR\",\n    \"coritiba-pr\": \"Coritiba\",\n    \"parana-pr\": \"Paraná\",\n    \"sport-pe\": \"Sport\",\n    \"santa-cruz-pe\": \"Santa Cruz\",\n    \"nautico-pe\": \"Náutico\",\n    \"bahia-ba\": \"Bahia\",\n    \"vitoria-ba\": \"Vitória-BA\",\n    \"ceara-ce\": \"Ceará\",\n    \"fortaleza-ce\": \"Fortaleza\",\n    \"goias-go\": \"Goiás\",\n    \"vila-nova-go\": \"Vila Nova\",\n    \"atletico-go\": \"Atlético-GO\",\n    \"avai-sc\": \"Avaí\",\n    \"chapecoense-sc\": \"Chapecoense\",\n    \"figueirense-sc\": \"Figueirense\",\n    \"criciuma-sc\": \"Criciúma\",\n    \"csa-al\": \"CSA\",\n    \"crb-al\": \"CRB\",\n    \"remo-pa\": \"Remo\",\n    \"paysandu-pa\": \"Paysandu\",\n    \"ipatinga-mg\": \"Ipatinga\",\n    \"barueri-sp\": \"Barueri\",\n    \"sao-jose-rs\": \"São José\",\n    \"sao-luiz-rs\": \"São Luiz\",\n    \"sao-bento-sp\": \"São Bento\",\n    \"sao-bernardo-sp\": \"São Bernardo\",\n    \"vitoria-da-conquista-ba\": \"Vitória da Conquista\",\n    \"novo-hamburgo-rs\": \"Novo Hamburgo\",\n    \"brasil-de-pelotas-rs\": \"Brasil de Pelotas\",\n    \"cuiaba-mt\": \"Cuiabá\",\n    \"atletico-ba\": \"Atlético-BA\",\n}\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Name cleaning\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n\n\ndef strip_accents(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return ``text`` with accents removed (São Paulo -> Sao Paulo).\"\"\"\n    return \"\".join(\n        ch\n        for ch in unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", text)\n        if not unicodedata.combining(ch)\n    )\n\n\ndef _clean_tokens(raw: str) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Lower-case, de-accent, drop parentheses/filler and club-form tokens.\"\"\"\n    text = strip_accents(raw).lower()\n    text = re.sub(r\"\\([^)]*\\)\", \" \", text)  # drop parenthetical remarks\n    text = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \" \", text)  # punctuation -> spaces\n    tokens = [t for t in text.split() if t not in _FILLER]\n    # Strip a single leading/trailing club-form token (\"EC Bahia\", \"Fortaleza FC\").\n    if len(tokens) > 1:\n        if tokens[0] in _FORM_TOKENS:\n            tokens = tokens[1:]\n        elif tokens[-1] in _FORM_TOKENS:\n            tokens = tokens[:-1]\n    return tokens\n\n\ndef team_key(raw: str, uf_hint: str | None = None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Compute the canonical team id for a raw name.\n\n    ``uf_hint`` is the state column value some datasets provide next to the\n    team name; it is only consulted when the name itself carries no\n    recognisable suffix.\n    \"\"\"\n    tokens = _clean_tokens(raw)\n    if not tokens:\n        return \"\"\n\n    uf = None\n    country = None\n    if tokens[-1] in BRAZILIAN_UFS:\n        uf = tokens[-1]\n        tokens = tokens[:-1]\n    elif tokens[-1] in FOREIGN_MARKERS and len(tokens) > 1:\n        country = tokens[-1]\n        tokens = tokens[:-1]\n    elif uf_hint:\n        hint = strip_accents(uf_hint).lower().strip()\n        if hint in BRAZILIAN_UFS:\n            uf = hint\n\n    if not tokens:  # the name was only a suffix\n        return (uf or country or \"\")\n\n    key = \"-\".join(tokens)\n    if uf:\n        key += f\"-{uf}\"\n    elif country:\n        key += f\"-{country}\"\n\n    return ALIASES.get(key, key)\n\n\ndef base_name(canonical: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the base name of a canonical id without its suffix, if any.\"\"\"\n    tokens = canonical.split(\"-\")\n    while len(tokens) > 1 and (\n        tokens[-1] in BRAZILIAN_UFS or tokens[-1] in FOREIGN_MARKERS\n    ):\n        tokens = tokens[:-1]\n    return \"-\".join(tokens)\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Registry\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n\n\nclass TeamNotFound(LookupError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a team name cannot be resolved to any known club.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ResolveResult:\n    \"\"\"Outcome of resolving a user-provided team name.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, canonical: str, display: str, alternatives: list[str]):\n        self.canonical = canonical\n        self.display = display\n        self.alternatives = alternatives\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:  # pragma: no cover - debug helper\n        return f\"<ResolveResult {self.canonical!r} alternatives={self.alternatives}>\"\n\n\nclass TeamRegistry:\n    \"\"\"Registry of every team seen in the datasets.\n\n    Built dynamically while loading: every raw spelling is converted to a\n    canonical key, then :meth:`merge_bare_keys` folds bare keys (no state\n    suffix) into their state-suffixed twin -- automatically when unique,\n    otherwise by prominence (match appearances).\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._counts: Counter[str] = Counter()  # canonical -> appearances\n        self._redirects: dict[str, str] = {}  # folded key -> final key\n        self._canonical_cache: dict[tuple[str, str], str] = {}\n        self._merged = False\n\n    # -- building ---------------------------------------------------------- #\n\n    def note(self, raw: str, uf_hint: str | None = None) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Record one appearance of a raw team name.\"\"\"\n        self._counts[team_key(raw, uf_hint)] += 1\n\n    def merge_bare_keys(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Fold bare keys (no state suffix) into state-suffixed twins.\n\n        ``\"palmeiras\"`` becomes ``\"palmeiras-sp\"`` because that is the only\n        club with that base; for ambiguous bases like ``\"santos\"`` the most\n        prominent twin wins (Santos FC of São Paulo, not Santos-AP).\n        \"\"\"\n        if self._merged:\n            return\n\n        # Apply curated aliases first so the folds operate on post-alias keys.\n        for key in list(self._counts):\n            target = ALIASES.get(key)\n            if target is not None and target != key:\n                self._counts[target] += self._counts.pop(key)\n                self._redirects[key] = target\n\n        bases: dict[str, list[str]] = {}\n        for canonical in self._counts:\n            bases.setdefault(base_name(canonical), []).append(canonical)\n\n        for base, twins in bases.items():\n            if len(twins) <= 1:\n                continue\n            bare = base  # the bare key form equals its own base name\n            suffixed = [t for t in twins if t != bare and t != base_name(t)]\n            if not suffixed:\n                continue\n            if bare in suffixed:  # defensive; cannot happen\n                continue\n            if len(suffixed) == 1:\n                target = suffixed[0]\n            else:\n                target = max(suffixed, key=lambda t: self._counts[t])\n            if bare in self._counts and self._counts[bare] <= self._counts.get(target, 0):\n                self._counts[target] += self._counts.pop(bare)\n                self._redirects[bare] = target\n\n        # Chase alias redirects through to the final key.\n        def _final(key: str) -> str:\n            seen = set()\n            while key in self._redirects and key not in seen:\n                seen.add(key)\n                key = self._redirects[key]\n            return key\n\n        self._redirects = {k: _final(v) for k, v in self._redirects.items()}\n        self._canonical_cache.clear()\n        self._merged = True\n\n    # -- canonicalisation --------------------------------------------------- #\n\n    def canonical(self, raw: str, uf_hint: str | None = None) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Final canonical id for a raw name (memoised).\n\n        Applies :func:`team_key`, curated aliases, merge redirects, and a\n        final base-name fold so bare spellings (\"Grêmio\") land on the\n        state-suffixed club actually present in the data (\"gremio-rs\").\n        Unknown/foreign names are returned unchanged.\n        \"\"\"\n        cache_key = (raw.strip().lower(), uf_hint or \"\")\n        if cache_key in self._canonical_cache:\n            return self._canonical_cache[cache_key]\n\n        key = team_key(raw, uf_hint)\n        key = self._redirects.get(key, key)\n        if key not in self._counts:\n            base = base_name(key)\n            twins = [\n                c for c in self._counts\n                if base_name(c) == base and c != key\n            ]\n            if len(twins) == 1:\n                key = twins[0]\n            elif twins:\n                key = max(twins, key=lambda t: self._counts[t])\n\n        self._canonical_cache[cache_key] = key\n        return key\n\n    def display(self, canonical: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Human-readable display name for a canonical id.\"\"\"\n        if canonical in _DISPLAY_OVERRIDES:\n            return _DISPLAY_OVERRIDES[canonical]\n        return canonical.replace(\"-\", \" \").title()\n\n    # -- querying ----------------------------------------------------------- #\n\n    def __contains__(self, canonical: str) -> bool:\n        return canonical in self._counts\n\n    def __len__(self) -> int:\n        return len(self._counts)\n\n    def known_teams(self) -> list[str]:\n        return sorted(self._counts)\n\n    def match_count(self, canonical: str) -> int:\n        return self._counts.get(canonical, 0)\n\n    def _base_index(self) -> dict[str, set[str]]:\n        bases: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n        for canonical in self._counts:\n            bases.setdefault(base_name(canonical), set()).add(canonical)\n        return bases\n\n    def _alternatives_for(self, canonical: str) -> list[str]:\n        base = base_name(canonical)\n        return [\n            self.display(other)\n            for other in self._counts\n            if other != canonical and base_name(other) == base\n        ][:4]\n\n    def resolve(self, query: str) -> ResolveResult:\n        \"\"\"Resolve a user-supplied team name to a canonical team.\n\n        Strategy (in order): exact canonical key -> unique base match ->\n        most prominent base match -> fuzzy substring / similarity match.\n        Raises :class:`TeamNotFound` when nothing is close enough.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not query or not query.strip():\n            raise TeamNotFound(\"Empty team name\")\n\n        key = self.canonical(query)\n        if key in self._counts:\n            return ResolveResult(key, self.display(key), self._alternatives_for(key))\n\n        q_base = base_name(key) if key else strip_accents(query).lower()\n        bases = self._base_index()\n        if q_base in bases:\n            twins = bases[q_base]\n            if len(twins) == 1:\n                canonical = next(iter(twins))\n                return ResolveResult(canonical, self.display(canonical), [])\n            ranked = sorted(twins, key=lambda t: -self._counts[t])\n            return ResolveResult(ranked[0], self.display(ranked[0]), ranked[1:5])\n\n        # Fuzzy: containment on base names, then similarity ranking.\n        candidates = {\n            canonical\n            for canonical in self._counts\n            if q_base in base_name(canonical) or base_name(canonical) in q_base\n        }\n        if not candidates:\n            lowered = strip_accents(query).lower()\n            candidates = {\n                canonical\n                for canonical in self._counts\n                if lowered in base_name(canonical) or base_name(canonical) in lowered\n            }\n        if not candidates:\n            matches = difflib.get_close_matches(\n                q_base, [base_name(c) for c in self._counts], n=5, cutoff=0.6\n            )\n            candidates = {c for c in self._counts if base_name(c) in matches}\n        if not candidates:\n            raise TeamNotFound(f\"No team matching {query!r} in the dataset\")\n\n        ranked = sorted(candidates, key=lambda t: (-self._counts[t], base_name(t)))\n        return ResolveResult(ranked[0], self.display(ranked[0]), ranked[1:5])\n\n    def search(self, query: str, limit: int = 10) -> list[dict]:\n        \"\"\"Search teams by name; returns display info for tooling/LLMs.\"\"\"\n        q_key = team_key(query)\n        q_base = base_name(q_key) if q_key else strip_accents(query).lower()\n        q_lower = strip_accents(query).lower()\n\n        scored: list[tuple[int, int, str]] = []\n        for canonical, count in self._counts.items():\n            base = base_name(canonical)\n            if base == q_base:\n                score = 100\n            elif q_base in base or base in q_base:\n                score = 60\n            elif q_lower in base or base in q_lower:\n                score = 40\n            else:\n                continue\n            scored.append((score, count, canonical))\n        scored.sort(key=lambda t: (-t[0], -t[1]))\n        return [\n            {\n                \"id\": canonical,\n                \"name\": self.display(canonical),\n                \"matches_in_dataset\": count,\n            }\n            for _score, count, canonical in scored[:limit]\n        ]\n","filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6/brazilian_soccer/normalize.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6/brazilian_soccer/normalize.py","exists":true,"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer/normalize.py","time":{"start":1788282747503,"end":1788282747518}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait, there's a bug in my `merge_bare_keys` logic. Let me re-check:\n\n```python\nsuffixed = [t for t in twins if t != bare and \"-\" in t[len(base):]]\n```\n\n`twin` for base \"palmeiras\" = [\"palmeiras\", \"palmeiras-sp\"]. `t[len(base):]` for \"palmeiras-sp\" = \"-sp\" which contains \"-\". OK. For base \"atletico mineiro\": twins would be [\"atletico mineiro\" (bare key with space!)]. Hmm — bare key \"atletico mineiro\" has a space, not a dash. My canonical keys use \"-\" as separator: \"atletico-mg\". The bare form of \"atletico-mg\" via base_name is \"atletico\" (no suffix stripped... wait base_name splits on \"-\" and strips trailing UF tokens: \"atletico-mg\" → tokens [\"atletico\",\"mg\"], \"mg\" in UFS → stripped → base \"atletico\").\n\nBut the bare key computed from raw \"Atletico Mineiro\" via team_key() is \"atletico mineiro\" (with SPACE — since I join tokens with \"-\" only... wait no: `key = \"-\".join(tokens)` — tokens [\"atletico\", \"mineiro\"] → \"atletico-mineiro\". Hmm! I join base tokens with \"-\" too. So bare key for \"Atletico Mineiro\" = \"atletico-mineiro\", and its base_name = \"atletico-mineiro\" (no UF suffix to strip). But \"atletico-mg\" has base_name \"atletico\". These bases DIFFER → no auto-merge → that's why ALIASES has \"atletico mineiro\"?? \n\nWait — inconsistency: in ALIASES I wrote keys with SPACES (\"atletico mineiro\") but team_key produces dashes (\"atletico-mineiro\")! Let me trace team_key(\"Atletico Mineiro\"): tokens [\"atletico\",\"mineiro\"]; last token \"mineiro\" not UF/marker → no uf/country; key = \"atletico-mineiro\" (join with \"-\"). So ALIASES keys must be \"atletico-mineiro\" not \"atletico mineiro\"!\n\nHmm wait, but then \"palmeiras-sp\" — tokens for \"Palmeiras-SP\" = [\"palmeiras\",\"sp\"] → last \"sp\" is UF → uf=\"sp\", tokens=[\"palmeiras\"] → key=\"palmeiras-sp\" ✓. And \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\": tokens [\"vasco\",\"da\",\"gama\",\"rj\"] → uf=\"rj\", base tokens [\"vasco\",\"da\",\"gama\"] → key \"vasco-da-gama-rj\". Hmm! But in ALIASES/_DISPLAY_OVERRIDES I wrote \"vasco da gama-rj\" (spaces). Inconsistent!\n\nI need to decide the canonical key format: dashes everywhere. So \"vasco-da-gama-rj\". And base_name(\"vasco-da-gama-rj\") = strip trailing UF tokens → \"vasco-da-gama\". A bare \"Vasco da Gama\" → key \"vasco-da-gama\" → base match \"vasco-da-gama\" == base of \"vasco-da-gama-rj\" ✓ auto-merge works.\n\nLet me fix all keys to use dashes consistently:\n- ALIASES keys: \"atletico-mineiro\", \"atletico-paranaense\", \"athletico-paranaense\", \"atletico-goianiense\", \"sport-recife\", \"sport-club-do-recife\", \"nautico-capibaribe\", \"ceara-sporting-club\", \"fortaleza-fc\", \"america-fc\", \"corinthians-paulista\", \"sport-club-corinthians-paulista\", \"botafogo-rj\" (from \"Botafogo RJ\" → tokens [\"botafogo\",\"rj\"] → uf rj → key \"botafogo-rj\" — auto exact match, no alias needed), \"america-mg\" (auto), \"cuiaba-mt\" (auto), \"gremio-rs\" (auto — \"Gremio RS\" → uf rs ✓), \"internacional-rs\"/\"internacional-sc\" (auto), \"fluminense-rj\"/\"fluminense-pi\" (auto), \"vasco-da-gama-rj\" (auto), \"vitoria-es\" (auto), \"red-bull-brasil-sp\"? \"Red Bull Brasil - SP\" → tokens [red,bull,brasil,sp] → uf sp → \"red-bull-brasil-sp\" auto ✓ (drop from aliases), \"rb-bragantino\"? tokens [rb,bragantino] → \"rb-bragantino\" — hmm \"RB Bragantino\" bare in BR-Football? It's \"Red Bull Bragantino\" there. \"RB Bragantino\" is used in some sources; keep alias \"rb-bragantino\" → \"red-bull-bragantino-sp\". \"brasil-de-pelotas-rs\"? BR-Football has \"Brasil de Pelotas\" bare → key \"brasil-de-pelotas\" → base unique? if \"brasil-de-pelotas-rs\" exists in Copa file → auto-merge handles; keep alias as backup. \"aguia-de-maraba-pa\" similar. \"nautico\" bare → alias to \"nautico-pe\" (unique UF twin; auto-merge would handle too since bare \"nautico\" appears only in FIFA (\"Nautico\"? no FIFA doesn't have it) — keep alias anyway harmless... wait, is it harmless? ALIASES maps \"nautico\"→\"nautico-pe\". If some obscure \"Náutico\" club from another state appeared bare in data, it'd wrongly merge. No such case. Keep.\n\nAlso note in ALIASES I have entries like \"atletico-pr\" → \"athletico-pr\": \"atletico-pr\" is a real key produced from data (\"Atlético-PR\" in Brasileirao file) — merging is CORRECT (same club, old spelling).\n\nAnd \"curitiba pr\": \"Coritiba PR\" (BR-Football) → tokens [coritiba, pr] → uf \"pr\" → key \"coritiba-pr\" auto ✓ — that alias entry is unnecessary; remove.\n\nAlso fix _DISPLAY_OVERRIDES keys to dashed format: \"vasco-da-gama-rj\", \"santo-andre-sp\", \"sao-caetano-sp\", \"red-bull-bragantino-sp\", \"brasil-de-pelotas-rs\" etc.\n\nAlso the merge_bare_keys logic: `if \"-\" in t[len(base):]` — for twin \"atletico-mg\" with base \"atletico\": t[len(base):] = \"-mg\" contains \"-\" ✓. For twin \"vasco-da-gama-rj\" base \"vasco-da-gama\": \"-rj\" ✓. OK but there's a subtle issue: twins list is built from bases dict — all canonicals with same base_name. The bare form to fold: bare == base. But bare KEY as computed by team_key for e.g. \"Grêmio\" = \"gremio\" — and base_name(\"gremio-rs\") = \"gremio\" ✓ match. For \"Atletico Mineiro\" bare key = \"atletico-mineiro\" — base_name of it = \"atletico-mineiro\" ≠ base_name(\"atletico-mg\")=\"atletico\". NOT auto-merged → alias \"atletico-mineiro\" → \"atletico-mg\" handles it ✓ (after I fix the alias key format).\n\nOne more subtlety in merge_bare_keys: `if bare not in self._counts` — the bare key exists in counts only if some data source used the bare spelling. E.g. Libertadores \"Palmeiras\" → key \"palmeiras\" ✓ present. Then suffixed twins from bases[\"palmeiras\"] = {\"palmeiras\", \"palmeiras-sp\"} → suffixed = [\"palmeiras-sp\"] → merge ✓.\n\nEdge: base \"santos\": twins {\"santos\", \"santos-sp\", \"santos-ap\"} → suffixed both → target = most prominent = santos-sp ✓.\n\nEdge: FIFA-only clubs: \"paris-saint-germain\" etc. — bare keys with no UF twins → stand alone ✓. But FIFA clubs are NOT noted into the registry counts in my loader plan... Actually should FIFA clubs be registered? For search_players club resolution, I resolve the club query via registry.resolve — \"Grêmio\" → \"gremio-rs\" ✓ then filter players by player.club canonical == gremio-rs. To do that, I need to canonicalize each player's club string via team_key (cheap, per unique club string ~600 clubs) — NOT via registry.note (would pollute match counts). I'll compute `club_key` per player with team_key(raw) then apply ALIASES. \"Grêmio\" → \"gremio\" bare → then for matching \"gremio-rj\"... I need club→canonical resolution against the registry. So in loader, after registry built & merged, map each unique raw FIFA club: key = team_key(raw); if key in registry → use it; else try ALIASES; else keep raw key as its own id (foreign clubs: \"juventus\", \"paris-saint-germain\" — fine as own ids; they don't collide with Brazilian teams).\n\nWait — bare \"gremio\" from FIFA: after registry merge, \"gremio\" bare was folded into \"gremio-rs\" in the REGISTRY, but the player's computed key is still \"gremio\". So player club canonicalization must ALSO apply the merge logic. Solution: registry exposes `canonicalize(key)` that returns the merged target for a bare key: after merges, I can store a mapping `_key_redirects: dict[bare_or_old_key, final_key]` updated during merges. Simplest: in merge_bare_keys, record every fold (old → new) into a `_redirects` dict; and expose `canonicalize(key)` = `_redirects.get(key, key)`. The player club canonicalization uses team_key then registry.canonicalize + ALIASES lookups.\n\nLet me restructure: `registry.canonicalize(raw, uf_hint=None) -> str` — computes team_key, applies ALIASES chain and _redirects. `note()` uses canonicalize too. And `resolve(query)` — computes team_key(query), canonicalize, then exact/base/fuzzy as before.\n\nAlso in resolve(): the `q_base` for a query with UF baked (\"Atlético-MG\") → key \"atletico-mg\" → in counts → exact ✓. Query \"Atletico\" → key \"atletico\" → not in counts (bare folded) → base match: bases[\"atletico\"] = {atletico-mg, atletico-go, athletico-pr?...}. Wait — base_name(\"athletico-pr\") = \"athletico\" (different spelling). So base \"atletico\" → {atletico-mg, atletico-go, atletico-ba?, atletico-es?} → ranked by count → atletico-mg + alternatives ✓.\n\nQuery \"Athletico\" → key \"athletico\" → folded? In data, \"Athletico\" bare appears (from collisions list: 'Athletico' appears as a raw variant!). Merged into athletico-pr (unique twin). So resolve via redirect: canonicalize(\"athletico\") → \"athletico-pr\" → in counts ✓ exact after redirect. \n\nNow rewrite normalize.py with these fixes:\n1. ALIASES keys with dashes.\n2. `canonicalize()` method + `_redirects` recorded during merges.\n3. `resolve()` uses canonicalize.\n4. Fix _DISPLAY_OVERRIDES keys to dashes.\n5. Remove bogus aliases (\"curitiba pr\", \"botafogo rj\", \"america mg\", \"america rn\", \"gremio rs\", \"internacional rs/sc\", \"fluminense rj/pi\", \"cuiaba\", \"cuiaba mt\", \"vasco da gama rj\", \"vitoria es\", \"fortaleza fc\"/\"fortaleza fc-ce\"? — \"Fortaleza FC\" → tokens [fortaleza] (fc stripped as trailing form token!) wait: _clean_tokens(\"Fortaleza FC\") → tokens [\"fortaleza\",\"fc\"] → len>1, tokens[-1] in _FORM_TOKENS → strip → [\"fortaleza\"] → key \"fortaleza\" → base match unique twin \"fortaleza-ce\" ✓ auto. So no alias needed. Similarly \"EC Bahia\" → [\"ec\",\"bahia\"] → leading \"ec\" stripped → \"bahia\" ✓.\n   Keep aliases only for structural mismatches: mineiro/paranaense/goianiense/recife/capibaribe/sporting-club/corinthians-paulista/rb-bragantino/america-fc-minas-gerais(→america-mg)/nautico-bare.\n\nLet me also double-check \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\": tokens: parens dropped → \"america fc\" → trailing fc stripped → [\"america\"] → key \"america\" → canonicalize redirect: bare \"america\" folded into most prominent \"america-mg\" during registry merge? Wait — is bare \"america\" in the MATCH data? From collisions: 'America - MG' etc all have UF. BR-Football \"America MG\" → tokens [america, mg] → uf → \"america-mg\" ✓. So bare \"america\" never appears in match data → no redirect recorded → player club key \"america\" stays → then resolve(\"america\")→ base match picks america-mg, but the player FILTER compares player.club_key == resolved club id (\"america-mg\") — player.club_key is \"america\" ≠ \"america-mg\" → MISS!\n\nHmm. So for player club filtering I need the same base-merge logic. Options:\na) Register FIFA club keys into the registry WITH zero match counts but with merges applied — i.e., note them with a \"players\" count? That pollutes match_count used for prominence.\nb) Give the registry a separate `_known` set; canonicalize() falls back: if key not in _redirects and not in _counts, apply base-resolution: find registry canonicals with same base_name → if exactly one → redirect to it; if several → most prominent. This is exactly resolve()'s base logic. I'll implement `canonicalize(key)` to do the full fold including base-prominence folding — deterministic, data-driven.\n\nSo `canonicalize(raw_or_key)`:\n```python\nkey = team_key(raw) if not is_key else raw\nkey = ALIASES.get(key, key)\nkey = self._redirects.get(key, key)\nif key in self._counts: return key\n# base fold\nbase = base_name(key)\ntwins = [c for c in self._counts if base_name(c) == base]  # O(n) — fine, called rarely\nif len(twins) == 1: return twins[0]\nif twins: return max(twins, key=counts)\nreturn key  # unknown/foreign — keep as-is\n```\n\nBut careful: this base-fold must NOT be applied when the key genuinely refers to a foreign club (e.g. \"santos-laguna\" base \"santos-laguna\" — no twins → stays ✓; \"juventus\" stays ✓). And \"nacional-uru\" → in counts (from Libertadores data) → exact ✓. Player club \"Vitória de Setúbal\" → \"vitoria-de-setubal\" → base \"vitoria-de-setubal\" no twins → stays ✓ (good, distinct from vitoria-ba).\n\nHmm wait, one more: bare \"vitoria\" from FIFA \"Vitória\" → base fold: twins {vitoria-ba, vitoria-es} → most prominent vitoria-ba ✓ (FIFA Vitória IS Vitória-BA ✓).\n\nAnd resolve(\"Vitoria\") → key \"vitoria\" → canonicalize → vitoria-ba ✓ then alternatives from base. \n\nBut careful with resolve(\"america\") for QUERIES: user might mean América-RN. resolve returns most prominent + alternatives ✓ good.\n\nc) Also apply canonicalize in note()? note() currently uses team_key directly and merging happens later in merge_bare_keys. I'll keep note() simple (raw key) + redirects recorded in merge; canonicalize consulted by loader for players and by resolve. But note() must ALSO return the final canonical — after merge_bare_keys, the counts are folded, so loader calls note() for every row BEFORE merge... the matches store canonical keys via note()'s return which is the PRE-merge key (e.g. \"palmeiras\" from Libertadores). After merge, those stored keys are stale!\n\nFix: loader stores MATCHES with raw key from note(), then calls merge_bare_keys(), THEN re-canonicalizes stored keys via `_redirects`. OR: two-pass — first pass: note all names (build registry), merge; second pass: build matches with canonicalize(). Two passes over 40K rows is cheap (note is memoized per raw+hint, and canonicalize is memoizable). Cleaner: \n- Pass 1: for each file, iterate rows, registry.note(raw, hint) — builds counts + raw cache.\n- merge_bare_keys() with redirects.\n- Pass 2: build Match objects using registry.canonicalize(raw, hint) — memoized via _raw_seen? I'll add `canonical_for(raw, hint)` memo dict separate from counts: raw+hint → final canonical.\n\nLet me restructure TeamRegistry API:\n- `note(raw, uf_hint)` — counts appearances (pre-merge), memoized raw→prekey.\n- `merge_bare_keys()` — folds, records `_redirects`.\n- `canonical(raw, uf_hint=None) -> final canonical` — memoized: team_key → ALIASES → _redirects → base-fold. Used in pass 2 and by resolve.\n\nActually simpler: keep ONE memo `_final_cache[(raw, hint)] -> final` used by canonical(); note() can just call team_key without caching (it's fast: regex per unique raw string is fine — 40K calls of regex ~ each maybe 20µs → 0.8s... hmm, memoize per unique raw+hint string: ~1500 unique → fast). I'll memoize with a dict.\n\nLet me also double check `_clean_tokens` for \"Operario Ferroviario Esporte C - PR\": → tokens [operario, ferreoviario... \"ferroviario\", esporte, c, pr] → uf \"pr\" → key \"operario-ferroviario-esporte-c-pr\". Fine, unique junk key. But wait — \"Esporte C\" truncated; also BR-Football calls the same club \"Operario PR\" → key \"operario-pr\"! Two DIFFERENT keys for the same club (Operário-PR, Série B). Ugh. Add aliases: \"operario-ferroviario-esporte-c-pr\" → \"operario-pr\"? But which is \"operario\"? There's also Operário-MS (\"Operario MS\" in BR-Football → \"operario-ms\"). The Copa full name is Operário Ferroviário EC of PR. Add alias \"operario-ferroviario-esporte-c-pr\" → \"operario-pr\". And FIFA? Not in FIFA. Fine — one curated alias. These small-club mismatches only matter for h2h/team queries on them; acceptable risk with curated alias for this one (Série B club, likely queried).\n\nSimilar truncations in Copa file: \"Operario Ferroviario Esporte C - PR\" is the one I saw. Let me scan for other truncated \"Esporte C\" names later during verification (spot-check some Série B clubs' match counts across files).\n\nOK — rewrite normalize.py now with all fixes. Also fix the weird difflib double-comprehension in resolve() fuzzy fallback (buggy: `for name in [next(...)]` with duplicate base names would mis-map). I'll simplify: collect (canonical) where base or key is close via difflib on base names with a mapping list.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05df4e46b00183x2jjZdMlotpy","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","messageID":"msg_05df3e090001sLTHWOecp21855"}}
