8-sig')):\n    names.add(r['home']); names.add(r['away'])\nfor n in sorted(names):\n    low = n.lower()\n    if any(w in low for w in ['vasco','santos','sao paulo','paulo','avai','vitoria','ceara','csa','abc','remo','paysandu','parana','bragantino','cuiaba','goias','coritiba','juventude','caxias']):\n        print(repr(n))\n\"","time":{"start":1788282913743,"end":1788282913746}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Two findings:\n\n1. **BR-Football dates are shifted +1 day** for many matches vs Brasileirao_Matches (e.g. 2019-04-27 vs 04-28). Same scores. This is the classic \"timezone-shifted date\" data issue. This breaks my date-based dedup: 5 opponents × ~2-3 matches each from BR-Football survive as duplicates with wrong dates.\n\n   Fix options:\n   a) Source-priority dedup: since BR-Football is the least authoritative for dates, dedup on (season, competition, home, away, home_goals, away_goals) WITHOUT date? But two-legged cup ties have same pairing+possibly same score... For league (round-robin) each (home, away, season) is unique — dedup on that is safe for leagues. For cups, same pairing happens (two legs) but usually different scores/dates.\n   b) Better: dedup key = (competition, season, home, away, goals) and keep earliest-loaded source (priority order already loads Brasileirao first). The date variance doesn't matter for identity. Risk: legitimately two different matches same season same pairing same score — in Copa do Brasil (two-legged ties with identical scores possible but rare — e.g. 1-0/1-0 happens!). Hmm.\n\n   Actually wait — should I even care about BR-Football Serie A/B rows that duplicate the dedicated files? What does BR-Football ADD? Corners/shots/attacks stats + 2023 seasons + Serie B/C. The duplication is only a problem for the unified match list. \n\n   Practical approach: dedup key WITHOUT exact date but with a date-tolerance: same fixture ±3 days? Getting fancy. Alternative: two-level dedup:\n   - Level 1 (exact): (date, home, away, goals, competition) — catches novo (dates match exactly).\n   - Level 2 (fuzzy): for matches from lower-priority sources, if (season, competition, home, away, home_goals, away_goals) seen already (from a higher-priority source) AND the date is within 3 days of the seen match's date → duplicate.\n\n   Wait, but actually — let me check: are BR-Football dates CONSISTENTLY shifted, or just sometimes? From the sample: every Flamengo BR-Football row was +1 day vs Brasileirao. And BR-Football Serie A 2019 = 380 rows = full season; 25 of 38 Flamengo matches deduped exactly (meaning those dates matched?? no wait — 38 Brasileirao + 13 BR-Football survived = only 13 BR-Football rows NOT exact-deduped. But if ALL dates were shifted, all 38 would survive!). Hmm — contradiction. Let me re-read: BR-Football 2019 Flamengo rows = 13 survived + 25 exact-deduped. So only SOME dates are shifted (+1). OK — so exact dedup catches most; the +1 shifted ones survive.\n\n   So my two-level dedup: after exact-key check fails, check (competition, season, home, away, home_goals, away_goals) with date within ±3 days → duplicate. For cup two-legs with identical scores: legs are typically ≥1 week apart → date tolerance excludes → safe. Edge: Copa do Brasil \"Round 1\" two legs days apart with identical scores — possible but rare (0-0/0-0 within a week). Acceptable risk? Hmm. Actually let me make tolerance ±1 day for safety (the observed shift is exactly +1). Two legs never 1-2 days apart. Use ±1.\n\n   Wait — actually also same-source duplicates: BR-Football 2021 Serie A has 491 rows (should be 380) — 111 extras. Within-source duplicates with +1 shifts? My dedup should apply within BR-Football too (same fixture same score within 1 day) ✓ level-2 catches.\n\n2. **BR-Football team names**: 203 names whose keys are unknown elsewhere — MOSTLY small clubs in Serie C/Copa do Brasil that legitimately don't appear in the dedicated files (they're fine as unique keys). But some are variants of KNOWN clubs: 'Botafogo SP' → botafogo-sp ✓ known? It says unknown elsewhere — hmm, Copa file has \"Botafogo - SP\"? The collision list earlier showed 'Botafogo SP' and 'Botafogo PB'... from BR-Football. And 'Ceara' → 'ceara' bare → should fold to 'ceara-ce' (in known? 'ceara-ce' comes from \"Ceará - CE\" in Copa? If Copa has \"Ceará-CE\"... earlier collision list showed 'Ceara-CE' hmm it showed 'Ceara', 'Ceara-CE', 'Ceará', 'Ceará - CE'. The bare ones from BR-Football/novo(FIFA?) — anyway with always-fold these resolve.\n   'Bragantino' bare → folds to... twins with base 'bragantino': 'bragantino-pa'? and 'bragantino-sp'? From collisions: 'Bragantino - PA' and 'Bragantino - SP' exist. Bare 'bragantino' (BR-Football 2014-2023) — which one? Red Bull Bragantino era (2019+) = SP; older Bragantino (2014-2018 in Serie B) = also SP (Bragantino-SP was in Série B until 2018, became RB in 2019). Bragantino-PA is a tiny club (Copa do Brasil). Most prominent twin = bragantino-sp ✓ correct fold. But wait — is 'bragantino' in BR-Football for 2014-2018 = Bragantino-SP ✓ same club family (CA Bragantino → RB Bragantino). Close enough; but BR-Football ALSO has 'Red Bull Bragantino' separately → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp'? Then Bragantino (2014-2018) and RB Bragantino (2019+) both map to... bare 'bragantino' folds to 'bragantino-sp' while 'Red Bull Bragantino' → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' — DIFFERENT keys for the same club lineage! For h2h queries \"Bragantino vs X\" the user gets one or the other... Acceptable: they ARE different named entities in the source. But better: alias 'bragantino-sp' → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp'? Hmm — but then 'bragantino' bare folds to 'bragantino-sp' then needs the redirect chain. My canonical() chases redirects ✓. I'll add ALIASES 'bragantino-sp': 'red-bull-bragantino-sp'?? Wait no — hold on. Actually, hmm: 'Bragantino' bare in BR-Football — appears in Serie B 2014-2018 (pre-RB). 'Red Bull Bragantino' in Serie A 2020+. Two spellings of the continuing club. Merging them is the RIGHT call for h2h/records (same club). Add alias 'bragantino-sp' → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' and bare 'bragantino' → fold via base to... base('bragantino') twins: bragantino-pa, bragantino-sp — prominence picks bragantino-sp → then alias → red-bull-bragantino-sp ✓ chain works since canonical() applies ALIASES at team_key level + redirects + base-fold... wait, the ALIASES application in canonical() is only `team_key` (which applies ALIASES to its computed key) + `_redirects`. The base-fold result is NOT re-aliased! Order: team_key(\"Bragantino\") → \"bragantino\" (no alias hit) → redirects none → not in counts → base-fold → twins {bragantino-pa, bragantino-sp} → prominence → \"bragantino-sp\" → return... but alias \"bragantino-sp\" → \"red-bull-bragantino-sp\" never applied! Fix: apply ALIASES again after base-fold in canonical(). Also resolve() similarly uses canonical() so ✓.\n\n   Let me also check: 'CA Parana' → hmm, BR-Football 'CA Parana'? That's Paraná Clube (\"CA\" = Clube Atlético). Key 'ca-parana' should alias to 'parana-pr'. And 'America FC Natal' → 'america-fc-natal' should alias to 'america-rn'. 'Clube Do Remo' → 'clube-do-remo' → alias 'remo-pa'. 'CS Sergipe' → 'cs-sergipe' → 'sergipe-se'? 'CS Alagoano' → 'cs-alagoano' → 'csa-al'! 'CSA' bare → fold csa-al ✓. 'Atletico Acreano' → 'atletico-acreano' — distinct club (Atlético Acreano)... vs 'atletico-ac' from Libertadores/Copa? \"Atlético - AC\" appeared in collisions! So 'atletico-ac' known, 'atletico-acreano' unknown → alias 'atletico-acreano' → 'atletico-ac'. 'Atletico Alagoinhas' → distinct (Atlético de Alagoinhas-BA, different from atletico-ba? Yes different clubs). Hmm.\n\n   This tail is long. Cost-benefit: these affect obscure Serie C/Copa clubs. For the BDD/demo scope, I'll add aliases for the ones that matter (Série A/B clubs + notable Serie C): csa-al via 'CS Alagoano', parana-pr via 'CA Parana', america-rn via 'America FC Natal', remo-pa via 'Clube Do Remo', atletico-ac via 'Atletico Acreano', bragantino chain. Others: unique keys, still queryable via search_teams.\n\n   Hmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: 'CSA' bare folds to 'csa-al' ✓ and 'CS Alagoano' → alias → 'csa-al' ✓ same club. \n\n   Also 'Botafogo SP' → 'botafogo-sp' — but is 'botafogo-sp' in known keys from other files? The check said unknown elsewhere — meaning the Copa file spells it differently (\"Botafogo - SP\"? that gives 'botafogo-sp'...). Hmm wait — \"Botafogo - SP\" → tokens [botafogo, sp] → uf sp → 'botafogo-sp'. It should BE known... unless the collision list's 'Botafogo SP' variants all came from BR-Football and Copa has \"Botafogo (Ribeirão Preto)\"? Whatever — 'botafogo-sp' as its own key is fine even if only BR-Football uses it; no conflict.\n\n   'Central SC' → 'central' — base 'central' vs known 'central-sc'? Collision list had 'Central - PE' and 'Central SC'! Central-PE (Central de Caruaru) vs Central-SC?? Hmm \"Central SC\" — Santa Catarina? BR-Football names... Actually 'Central SC' might be Central Sport Club of PE mislabeled. Bare 'central' folds by prominence among central-pe/central-sc. Edge case, leave it.\n\n3. **Vasco**: BR-Football \"Vasco RJ\"?? Earlier 2019 store showed 'vasco-rj' 19 rows. And now the unknown-keys list doesn't show Vasco... The 203 list is truncated at 60. Let me get the full list and also find BR-Football names that DO map to known keys vs those creating split identities for prominent clubs. Let me check specifically for the 20 Série A 2019 clubs how BR-Football spells them.\n\nActually, more robust: instead of eyeballing, let me write the fixes (always-fold + post-fold alias + dedup ±1 day + priority) then run a standings sanity check for 2012-2022 seasons and compare champions with real history. Known champions: 2012 Fluminense, 2013 Cruzeiro, 2014 Cruzeiro, 2015 Corinthians, 2016 Palmeiras, 2017 Corinthians, 2018 Palmeiras, 2019 Flamengo, 2020 Flamengo, 2021 Atlético-MG, 2022 Palmeiras. If my stand