Dataset.csv\nnovo: 'Vitória' ES vs 'Internacional' RS\nnovo: 'Náutico' PE vs 'Vitória' BA\nnovo: 'Vitória' ES vs 'Vasco' RJ\nnovo: 'Grêmio' RS vs 'Vitória' BA\nnovo: 'Vitória' ES vs 'Athletico-PR' PR\nnovo: 'Goiás' GO vs 'Vitória' BA\nnovo: 'Vitória' ES vs 'São Paulo' SP\nnovo: 'Vitória' ES vs 'Bahia' BH\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6 && venv/bin/python -c \"\nfrom brazilian_soccer import load_soccer_data\nfrom brazilian_soccer.loader import BRASILEIRAO_A\nd = load_soccer_data()\nfor season, phantom in [(2013, 'vitoria-es'), (2020, 'bragantino-pa')]:\n    print(f'--- {season} {phantom}:')\n    for m in d.matches:\n        if m.competition == BRASILEIRAO_A and m.season == season and (m.home == phantom or m.away == phantom):\n            print(f'   {m.date} {m.home} {m.home_goals}-{m.away_goals} {m.away} src={m.source}')\n            break\n\" && venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\n# find raw rows producing vitoria-es in 2013: novo with wrong UF? or novo name 'Vitória' with UF ES\nfor r in csv.DictReader(open('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig')):\n    if r['Ano']=='2013' and ('Vit' in r['Equipe_mandante'] or 'Vit' in r['Equipe_visitante']):\n        print('novo:', repr(r['Equipe_mandante']), r['Mandante_UF'], 'vs', repr(r['Equipe_visitante']), r['Visitante_UF'])\n\" | head -8","time":{"start":1788283036819,"end":1788283036822}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I can see the remaining problems:\n\n1. **\"Vitoria Es\"** with 19 matches in 2013 — BR-Football 2013?? No wait, BR-Football starts 2014. Hmm — \"Vitoria Es\" 19 matches in 2013... which source? Vitória-BA played 2013 Série A. \"Vitoria Es\" key = \"vitoria-es\"! The alias... wait, novo 2013 has \"Vitória\" + hint BA → \"vitoria-ba\". \"Vitoria Es\" — that raw appears in BR-Football (\"Vitoria ES\")... but BR-Football has no 2013. Let me check: maybe novo 2013 has \"Vitória\" with Visitante_UF=\"ES\"?? Or... hold on, \"Vitoria Es\" displayed — display for \"vitoria-es\" → title case. 19 matches = HALF a season. In 2013, Vitória-BA played 38. So ONE FILE spells Vitória-BA as \"Vitória\" + hint \"ES\" wrongly?? The novo UF columns might have errors, or... let me find the source rows.\n\n2. **Teams with 39 matches in 2013** (vs 38): ONE extra match per team + \"Vitoria Es\" 19 + one more team at 20? Sum: 20 teams×38 + ... 399 = 380 + 19. Ahh — 21 teams: 20 real + \"Vitoria Es\" phantom with 19. The phantom pairs with real teams giving them +1 each?? No — 20 teams at 39 = +1 each; phantom 19 → total extra = 19+20 = 39... but matches=399 → extra 19 over 380. Consistent: phantom has 19 matches (one round-robin half), and 19 opponents each get +1... wait 20 teams at 39: that's +19 spread + 1 team at 39 unexplained? Listed: 19 teams at 39 + Fortaleza? no. 2013 teams list: 19 teams with 39 + Vitoria Es 19 + ... 21 teams total; one team not shown (38): count = 19×39 + 1×38 + 1×19 = 741+38+19 = 798/2 = 399 ✓. So the phantom \"Vitoria Es\" appears in 19 matches, and 19 teams got +1, one team stayed 38 (the phantom's 19 opponents are 19 of the 20 teams; the phantom is playing AGAINST Vitória-BA itself?? No — if phantom replaced vitoria-ba in 19 matches, vitoria-ba would have 19 too. Which team has 38? The list shows only ≠38 teams; the missing 38-team... vitoria-ba? NOT in the bad list → vitoria-ba has 38 ✓. So phantom \"Vitoria Es\" has its own 19 matches where the OPPONENT (each once) is one of the other teams, and the phantom is likely a MIS-NORMALIZED Vitória-BA in ONE file's AWAY rows: \"Vitória\" + hint \"ES\"?? Hmm no...\n\nLet me just find rows with home/away == 'vitoria-es' in 2013 and see the source + raw.\n\n3. **2020: \"Bragantino Pa\" 27** — phantom \"bragantino-pa\" (from BR-Football \"Bragantino PA\" — hmm wait, 2020?? BR-Football 2020 Serie A... \"Bragantino PA\" in 2020?! RB Bragantino played 2020. BR-Football apparently lists some Bragantino 2020 matches as \"Bragantino PA\" (wrong team or wrong rows). And other teams at 39-40 (extras from the 2020 BR-Football partial 264 rows that have date-shifts >1 day or score mismatches).\n\nHmm wait — 2020 extras: 407-380 = 27 = exactly the \"Bragantino Pa\" count. And 20 teams at 39-40 — the phantom's 27 matches inflate opponents. So 2020's problem is ONLY the phantom bragantino-pa. Where does \"Bragantino PA\" in 2020 come from? BR-Football \"Serie A\" 2020 rows with home/away \"Bragantino PA\". Those are REAL RB Bragantino matches but spelled \"Bragantino PA\" → key \"bragantino-pa\" ≠ \"red-bull-bragantino-sp\" → not deduped → duplicate entries for RB Bragantino matches (which also exist from Brasileirao_Matches as \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" → \"red-bull-bragantino-sp\").\n\nSo fix: alias \"bragantino-pa\" → \"red-bull-bragantino-sp\"?? WAIT — careful! Is BR-Football's \"Bragantino PA\" actually RB Bragantino or the tiny Bragantino-PA club (Bragantino de Pará?? there's \"Bragantino - PA\" in the Copa collisions list too!). Hmm. BR-Football Serie A 2020 with \"Bragantino PA\" — RB Bragantino (Série A 2020) is from SP. The \"PA\" here seems like a data error in BR-Football (or they mean Red Bull Bragantino mislabeled with PA state). Since it's in Série A 2020, it must be RB Bragantino (the Pará club never played Série A). But the Copa file \"Bragantino - PA\" (Copa do Brasil appearances) IS likely the Pará club.\n\nUgh — same spelling, two different clubs depending on context. My alias would wrongly merge the Copa Pará club into RB Bragantino. How to handle context-sensitively?\n\nOption: in `_row_to_matches` for BR-Football, alias \"Bragantino PA\" → Red Bull Bragantino (since in BR-Football, \"Bragantino\" and \"Bragantino PA\" both = RB Bragantino — the PA one is their error). And the Copa file's \"Bragantino - PA\" → keep as bragantino-pa (the tiny club). This means context-dependent normalization — I'll handle it as a per-file override map in the loader: BR_FOOTBALL_TEAM_OVERRIDES = {\"Bragantino PA\": \"Red Bull Bragantino\", ...}.\n\nWait — but actually, hold on: does the Copa file's \"Bragantino - PA\" refer to the Pará club? Hmm, \"Bragantino-PA\"... There IS a club \"Bragantino Clube do Pará\" (from Bragança, PA). Yes it exists and played Copa do Brasil. Keep it separate. ✓ So per-file override for BR-Football only.\n\n4. **2023 (BR-Football only): 377 matches, several teams at 37** — the data is partial (missing matches). Grêmio top at 68 — with 37 matches for some teams, the table is skewed. Real 2023: Palmeiras champion. The BR-Football 2023 data seems partial AND possibly wrong (Grêmio top). Hmm wait — actually, let me reconsider: maybe BR-Football 2023 \"Serie A\" is missing Palmeiras matches specifically (Palmeiras 37). With partial data the computed champion differs from reality. That's a DATA limitation, not a code bug. My `standings` tool should include a \"note\" when the season's data is incomplete (e.g., not all teams have equal match counts, or match count < expected). I'll add a note field: \"Partial data: N matches (expected ~380 for a 20-team double round-robin); standings may be unreliable.\"\n\nSo for tests, I'll assert champions only for 2012-2022 (well-covered seasons), and treat 2023 as partial. The TASK's example \"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023?\" still works as a query on partial data.\n\nWait — but what about \"Vitoria Es\" 2013? Let me find its source. And check 2014-2019, 2022 similar issues. Let me write a comprehensive per-season audit that flags phantom teams (match count far from the mode) and non-uniform counts, then fix systematically.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05df99996001fAB6Y1aPbtbjMe","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","messageID":"msg_05df9278d001it0rs4XmdPmhu1"}}
