n\n        # Pass 1: register every team name so the registry can merge bare\n        # spellings into state-suffixed canonical ids before matches are built.\n        for filename in match_files:\n            for row in self._read(filename):","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6/brazilian_soccer/loader.py","text":"    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ #\n\n    def _read(self, filename: str) -> list[dict]:\n        path = self.data_dir / filename\n        with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8-sig\", newline=\"\") as fh:\n            return list(csv.DictReader(fh))\n\n    def _load(self) -> None:\n        match_files = (\n            \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\",\n            \"Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\",\n            \"Libertadores_Matches.csv\",\n            \"novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv\",\n            \"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\",\n        )\n\n        # Pass 1: register every team name so the registry can merge bare\n        # spellings into state-suffixed canonical ids before matches are built.\n        for filename in match_files:\n            for row in self._read(filename):\n                self._note_teams(filename, row)\n\n        self.teams.merge_bare_keys()\n\n        # Pass 2: build the unified match store.  Two levels of dedup:\n        #   1. exact natural key (date + teams + score + competition);\n        #   2. same fixture (season + competition + teams + score) within\n        #      one calendar day -- some sources carry a one-day-shifted\n        #      date for the very same match (timezone artefacts).\n        seen: set[tuple] = set()\n        fuzzy_index: dict[tuple, list[Match]] = defaultdict(list)\n        for filename in match_files:\n            for row in self._read(filename):\n                for match in self._row_to_matches(filename, row):\n                    key = self._dedup_key(match)\n                    if key in seen:\n                        continue\n                    seen.add(key)\n                    fuzzy_key = (\n                        match.competition,\n                        match.season,\n                        match.home,\n                        match.away,\n                        match.home_goals,\n                        match.away_goals,\n                    )\n                    if any(\n                        match.date is not None\n                        and other.date is not None\n                        and abs((match.date - other.date).days) <= 1\n                        for other in fuzzy_index[fuzzy_key]\n                    ):\n                        continue\n                    fuzzy_index[fuzzy_key].append(match)\n                    self._add_match(match)\n\n        self._load_players()\n\n    def _note_teams(self, filename: str, row: dict) -> None:\n        if filename == \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\":","lineStart":108,"lineEnd":167,"totalLines":347,"truncated":true}},"title":"brazilian_soccer/loader.py","time":{"start":1788283120202,"end":1788283120209}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Hmm — the pattern is more complex:\n- Copa do Brasil has Jan/Feb rows in MANY years (2015, 2017-2020, 2022, 2023) — but Copa do Brasil FIRST ROUND legitimately happens in January/February! (Copa do Brasil 2018 first round: Jan-Feb ✓ real). So Copa Jan/Feb rows are LEGITIMATE with their label-year season.\n- Serie A 2021: 111 Jan/Feb = 2020 COVID tail (reassign to 2020).\n- Serie B 2021: 67 Jan = 2020 tail.\n- Serie C 2021: 13 Jan = 2020 tail.\n- Serie A 2016: 1 Jan row — hmm, Série A 2016? One stray row. Série A 2016 ran May-Dec... 1 row in Jan 2016? Junk or data error. Only 1 row — negligible, but my rule would move it to season 2015. Hmm. Let me look at that row.\n\nSo the rule can't be blanket Jan/Feb → year-1. It's LEAGUE-specific COVID spillover: only Serie A/B/C label-2021 Jan/Feb rows → season 2020. Copa Jan/Feb rows keep their year.\n\nIs it safe to hardcode \"league rows with label 2021 and month 01/02 → season 2020\"? The evidence: Série B 2021 real season was May-Dec (379 rows); Série A 2021 May-Dec (380); Série C 2021... ended Nov. And Série A 2020 was 264 rows + 111 tail = 375 ≈ complete 2020 ✓. It all fits COVID 2020 spillover into Jan-Feb 2021. For Serie A 2016's single Jan row — let me inspect it. If it's junk I'll leave it (1 row in a season covered by Brasileirao_Matches → skipped by source priority anyway!). YES — Série A 2016 is covered by Brasileirao_Matches (priority) so that stray row is dropped. Only BR-Football-only data matters: Série B/C (all years) + Série A 2023 + Copa 2022-2023.\n\nFor Série B/C label-2021 Jan rows → reassign to 2020. The stray Série A 2016 Jan row → skipped by priority anyway. Copa Jan rows → keep year.\n\nHmm — but wait, Série B 2020 (label 2020) = 302 rows; with the 67 reassigned = 369 ≈ 380 ✓. Série C 2020 = 179 + 13 = 192 ✓. Série A 2020 = 264 + 111 = 375 ✓ (but Série A 2020 is priority-covered, so reassigned rows get skipped — hmm!! WAIT. If I reassign Série A label-2021 Jan/Feb rows to season 2020, then for (Série A, 2020) the sources are Brasileirao_Matches (380, priority 0) + BR-Football (375, priority 2) → priority picks Brasileirao ✓ all BR-Football 2020 rows skipped ✓. And (Série A, 2021): Brasileirao (380) vs BR-Football (380 real May-Dec) → priority Brasileirao ✓ junk never enters. \n\nAnd Série B 2020: only BR-Football (302+67=369 after reassignment) ✓. Série B 2021: 379 real ✓.\n\nSo implementation: in `_row_to_matches` for BR-Football:\n```python\nseason = parse_year(date[:4])\nif month in (1,2) and tournament in {Serie A, Serie B, Serie C} and season == 2021:\n    season = 2020\n```\nGeneralize slightly: for LEAGUE tournaments, if month in (1,2) → season = year - 1?? For Série A 2016 Jan row that would give 2015 (covered by priority → skipped) — actually equivalent outcome. And there are no other league Jan/Feb rows. I'll use the generalized league rule: league + Jan/Feb → year-1. Hmm, but is that right conceptually? Brazilian national leagues have NEVER started before late January in this era... Actually Série A/B/C always start March/April or later; January/February matches in these datasets can only be spillover from the previous season. The generalized rule is principled: \"a league fixture played in Jan/Feb belongs to the season that started the previous year.\" For 2020→Jan/Feb 2021 it's exactly right; no other league Jan/Feb rows exist. Safe.\n\nAlso handle: the Série A 2016 Jan 2016 row → season 2015 → skipped (priority) ✓ no harm.\n\nNow implement everything:\n\n1. novo: drop UF hints (name-suffix + base-fold is more reliable).\n   WAIT — careful! Without hints, novo \"Bahia\" → \"bahia\" → fold → bahia-ba ✓. But novo ALSO has \"Bahia de Feira\" (Série B years? \"Bahia de Feira\" is a different club — base \"bahia-de-feira\" distinct ✓). And \"América-MG\"/\"América-RN\" baked ✓. \"Athletico-PR\"/\"Atlético-GO\"/\"Atlético-MG\" baked ✓ — good. But hmm — \"Atlético-GO\" baked + alias... no alias for that ✓. novo \"Sport\" bare → sport-pe ✓. novo \"Guarani\" bare → guarani-sp ✓. novo \"Vitória\" bare → vitoria-ba ✓ (fold). novo \"Grêmio\" → gremio-rs ✓. novo \"Paraná\" → parana-pr ✓. novo \"Náutico\" → nautico-pe ✓. novo \"Paysandu\" → paysandu-pa ✓. novo \"Santa Cruz\" → santa-cruz-pe ✓ (twins: santa-cruz-pe, santa-cruz-rn, santa-cruz-rs → prominence pe ✓). novo \"Juventude\" → juventude-rs ✓. novo \"Ceará\" → ceara-ce ✓. novo \"CSA\" → csa-al ✓. novo \"Criciúma\" → criciuma-sc ✓. novo \"Fortaleza\" → fortaleza-ce ✓. novo \"Goiás\" → goias-go ✓. novo \"Coritiba\" → coritiba-pr ✓. novo \"Ponte Preta\" → ponte-preta-sp ✓. novo \"Portuguesa\" → portuguesa-sp ✓ (vs portuguesa-rj → prominence sp ✓). novo \"Santo André\" → santo-andre-sp ✓. novo \"São Caetano\" → sao-caetano-sp ✓. novo \"Joinville\" → joinville-sc ✓. novo \"Ipatinga\" → ipatinga-mg ✓. novo \"Brasiliense\" → brasiliense-df ✓. novo \"Barueri\" → barueri-sp? twins: barueri-sp (from \"Barueri - SP\" in Copa?) hmm if no twin, bare \"barueri\" stands — fine either way. novo \"São Paulo\" → sao-paulo-sp ✓. novo \"Santos\" → santos-sp ✓. novo \"Palmeiras\" → palmeiras-sp ✓. novo \"Corinthians\" → corinthians-sp ✓. novo \"Flamengo\" → flamengo-rj ✓. novo \"Fluminense\" → fluminense-rj ✓. novo \"Botafogo-RJ\" baked ✓. novo \"Vasco\" → alias vasco → vasco-da-gama-rj ✓ (added). novo \"Cruzeiro\" → cruzeiro-mg ✓. novo \"Internacional\" → internacional-rs ✓. novo \"Avaí\" → avai-sc ✓. novo \"Chapecoense\" → chapecoense-sc ✓. novo \"Figueirense\" → figueirense-sc ✓. novo \"Grêmio Prudente\" → gremio-prudente — distinct club ✓ (base \"gremio-prudente\" ≠ \"gremio\" ✓).\n\n   All good. Drop hints for novo.\n\n2. BR-Football name override: \"Bragantino PA\" → \"Red Bull Bragantino\" (only within BR-Football parsing).\n\n3. BR-Football league Jan/Feb → season-1.\n\n4. Source priority per (competition, season).\n\n5. League pairing-uniqueness (Série A + B only — keep Série C exempt due to group formats... hmm wait, actually now that the Jan spillover is fixed, does Série C 2021 still have duplicate pairings? Série C had 13 reassigned to 2020 → Série C 2021 = 216 rows. Série C 2021 format: 20 clubs, 2 groups of 10, double round-robin within group (18 matches each = 180 total) + playoffs (~20 more) = ~200-216 ✓ plausible WITHOUT same-orientation pair repeats... group stage = same-orientation pairs once. Playoffs could repeat a pairing with same orientation (home/away legs are opposite orientations; a group-stage pairing could repeat in playoff with same orientation? e.g. Team A hosted in group, then A hosts first leg of playoff vs same team → same orientation twice legitimately!). Hmm. But it's rare. Risk assessment: Série C data is partial (1807/10 seasons); keeping pairing-uniqueness would drop legit playoff repeats; NOT having it keeps possible junk. Since Série C data is