data shows Série B 2017: 1 row only, Série C 2017: 16 rows. And 2018: Série C 22 rows. Hmm — Bragantino in Série C 2018?? No... whatever, this is BR-Football data quirk territory; Série C 2017-2018 rows for \"Bragantino\" might be the Pará club actually! Bragantino-PA played Série C 2017-2018? Hmm, no — Série C 2017-2018 \"Bragantino\"... The Pará club (Bragantino Clube do Pará) — I don't think they were in Série C 2017/2018. CA Bragantino-SP was in Série B both years...\n\nUgh, this is a rabbit hole. Decision: the Série B/A championship correctness matters (Série B 2019 champion = RB Bragantino ✓ real). The alias 'bragantino' → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' fixes Série B 2019/A 2020-2023. For Série C 2017-2018 rows — they'd fold to RB too; if they're actually the Pará club, minor misattribution in a partial Série C dataset. Acceptable.\n\nOption B: change ALIASES so 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' doesn't exist and everything is 'bragantino-sp'... The dedicated Copa file has BOTH \"Bragantino - PA\" (Pará) and \"Red Bull Bragantino - SP\". So keys 'bragantino-pa' and 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' both exist from the Copa file. Simplest: alias bare 'bragantino' → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp'.\n\nLet me also remove the BR_FOOTBALL_NAME_OVERRIDES (it was based on my wrong guess that \"Bragantino PA\" in BR-Football meant RB; actually it's fine either way — the override maps BR-Football \"Bragantino PA\" (Copa 2019/2020, 4 rows) to RB. Is that right? BR-Football Copa 2019 \"Bragantino PA\" — the DEDICATED Copa file 2019 — let me check what the dedicated file calls the same matches. If the dedicated file calls those fixtures \"Bragantino - PA\" then it's the Pará club and my override is WRONG. Let me check the dedicated Copa file for Bragantino 2019.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dfcdf85001J9GSEc7QL4apgu","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","messageID":"msg_05dfc7104001JZSrFjN5nyoS69"}}
