he source data!). Similarly 2019-04-20: \"Bragantino - PA 2-1 Bragantino - PA\" — BR-Football shows the same date as \"Bragantino PA 2-1 Vila Nova\"... hmm, no wait: BR-Football 2019-04-20 says \"Bragantino PA 2.0 - 1.0 Vila Nova\" but dedicated file says 2019-04-20 \"Bragantino - PA 2-1 Bragantino - PA\". Conflicting garbage.\n\nCross-referencing real history: Copa do Brasil 2019: Bragantino-PA (Pará club, \"Bragantino Clube do Pará\"?) hmm... Actually the real 2019 CdB second phase had \"Bragantino-PA vs Bragantino-SP\"?? I recall RB Bragantino played CdB 2019 and faced... The real fixture list: 2019 CdB second round: Bragantino-PA 2-0 Bragantino-PA is nonsense; the actual matches: RB Bragantino played Vila Nova? BR-Football says 2019-04-17 Vila Nova 2-0 Bragantino PA and 2019-04-20 Bragantino PA 2-1 Vila Nova — a two-legged tie Bragantino vs Vila Nova (aggregate 3-2 for Bragantino). And Juventude 0-0 Bragantino (2019-05-07) — BR-Football doesn't show those. The dedicated file has Juventude vs Bragantino too.\n\nSo BOTH spellings in both files refer to a mix of RB Bragantino (SP) and possibly the Pará club, and the sources contradict each other. This is deep data-quality mud. \n\nPRAGMATIC DECISION: \n- The alias 'bragantino' → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' for bare spellings (fixes Série B 2019 champion and Série A 2020-2023).\n- Keep 'bragantino-pa' as the key for explicit \"-PA\" spellings (dedicated Copa file). Whether those rows are RB or Pará club is unknowable from this data — Copa do Brasil early rounds only, minimal impact on h2h/records for famous clubs.\n- REMOVE my BR_FOOTBALL_NAME_OVERRIDES for \"Bragantino PA\" → since BR-Football's \"Bragantino PA\" Copa 2019 rows seem to mirror the dedicated file's \"Bragantino - PA\" (same fixtures: 2019-02-13 Bragantino PA 1-0 ASA matches dedicated 2019-02-13 Bragantino-PA 1-0 Asa-AL ✓ SAME fixture). So \"Bragantino PA\" in BR-Football = \"Bragantino - PA\" in dedicated file → same key 'bragantino-pa' ✓ consistent. Remove the override entirely.\n\nThen: base fold for bare 'bragantino' — twins: base 'bragantino' → {'bragantino-pa'} + 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' has base 'red-bull-bragantino' (different). So bare 'bragantino' would STILL fold to bragantino-pa! UNLESS the explicit alias 'bragantino' → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' fires in team_key BEFORE the fold. team_key returns ALIASES.get('bragantino', 'bragantino') — I add ALIASES['bragantino'] = 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' → bare 'bragantino' → team_key returns 'red-bull-bragantino-sp' directly ✓ never folds to bragantino-pa. \n\nBut WAIT — team_key applies ALIASES to the key for ANY occurrence of bare 'bragantino', including... that's what we want.\n\nHmm, but one more: the dedicated Copa file rows \"Bragantino - PA 2-0 Bragantino - PA\" (self-fixture nonsense) → both sides 'bragantino-pa' → match bragantino-pa vs bragantino-pa. Harmless garbage kept for cup completeness (rare). Could filter home==away matches as junk? A team never plays itself — good validation rule! Drop matches where home == away. That kills the nonsense rows. Let me add that to the dedup pipeline.\n\nSo:\n1. Remove BR_FOOTBALL_NAME_OVERRIDES entirely (revert _br_name usage — just use raw).\n2. Add ALIASES 'bragantino' → 'red-bull-bragantino-sp'.\n3. Drop self-play matches (home == away).\n\nThen re-validate Série B.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dfd60ab0016yVrowiRsf4PD5","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","messageID":"msg_05dfcea77001Nbps255IECd3IV"}}
