 \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  }\n]","metadata":{"todos":[{"content":"Write brazilian_soccer package: models.py, normalize.py (team registry)","status":"completed","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write loader.py (6 CSVs, dedup, unified match store)","status":"completed","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write queries.py (match/team/player/competition/stats queries)","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write server.py (MCP stdio server with 19 tools)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write BDD test suite (conftest + 8 test modules)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Run pytest, fix issues, verify MCP e2e + performance criteria","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write requirements.txt, update README with usage","status":"pending","priority":"medium"}],"truncated":false},"title":"5 todos","time":{"start":1788283397949,"end":1788283397950}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Outstanding: **Série A 11/11 champions correct** with exactly 380 matches per season, Série B 7/8. The only miss: Série B 2023 shows Vitória (72) vs expected Botafogo. Real 2023 Série B: champion Botafogo? Hmm — actually 2023 Série B champion was... Botafogo? No! 2023 Série B champion: Botafogo-SP?? No... Let me recall: Série B 2023 champion was Botafogo (SP)? NO — the 2023 Série B champion was Botafogo! Hmm, Botafogo (RJ) played Série B in 2023 (relegated from A in 2022) and... they finished?? Actually in 2023 Série B: champion was Botafogo? Hmm no wait — Botafogo won Série B 2023?? Hmm... No! Botafogo-RJ in 2023 played SÉRIE A?? No no. Botafogo-RJ was relegated in 2021? No... 2022 Série A Botafogo finished 12th; 2023 Série A Botafogo led the league most of the year (famous collapse). So Botafogo-RJ was in Série A 2023, NOT Série B!\n\n2023 Série B champion: Vitória! Hmm — no wait... Let me think properly: Série B 2023 final table: 1st place... The champion was Vitória-BA? Hmm, actually I'm now fairly sure 2023 Série B winner... no — 2023 Série B: champion was Botafogo-SP? Cuiabá? Grêmio? no (Grêmio was A). Let me recall news: \"Vitória retorna à elite... campeão da Série B 2023\"? Hmm, I genuinely remember Vitória got promoted to Série A 2024 (they finished 2nd?) and the champion was... hmm.\n\nActually: Série B 2023 standings: 1. Botafogo-SP?? No... I'm quite sure now: the 2023 Série B champion was Botafogo (SP)?? Hmm, no — Botafogo-SP finished like 10th.\n\nLet me look at real data: 2023 Série B promoted: Vitória (champion!), Juventude, Criciúma, Atlético-GO? The 2023 Série B champion WAS Vitória-BA (72 points?? hmm — Vitória 2023: champion with 72 points — hmm, I do recall Vitória winning Série B 2023 with a big season). Actually YES — my data says Vitória 72 pts. Let me sanity check with another source memory: 2023 Série B: Vitória champion (returned to Série A after 5 years). Juventude 2nd. Criciúma 3rd... Hmm, but wait, that doesn't match either — I thought Criciúma won?? No — Criciúma won Série B 2023?? Gah.\n\nActually, quick reasoning: my computed table has FULL data (380 matches, source BR-Football only, self-consistent). Trust: 2023 Série B champion per my data = Vitória. Real-world check: \"Campeonato Brasileiro Série B 2023\" — champion: Vitória! I'm now ~85% sure (Vitória had a dominant 2023 Série B with 20+ wins). Hmm — but wait, my expected list said Botafogo which I made up... Actually no — 2023 Série B: Botafogo?? I confused myself. Botafogo-RJ won Série B in 2021 ✓ (my data agrees). For 2023, the champion... Let me verify with known promoted teams 2024 Série A: Vitória, Juven... hmm — actually promoted from Série B 2023: Vitória, Juventude, Criciúma, Atlético-GO? Hmm no — 2024 Série A had: Vitória ✓ (yes, they were in Série A 2024), Criciúma ✓, Juventude ✓, Atlético-GO?? Cuiabá?? The four promoted in 2023: Vitória, Juventude, Criciúma + one more (Atlético-GO? no — Atlético-GO was RELEGATED from A in 2023). The 4th promoted: Cuiabá? no (Cuiabá was in A 2023). Bahia? no. It was... Vasco? no (Vasco promoted 2022). Hmm — 4th: Novorizontino? no. Sport? no — Sport promoted 2024. Hmm — actually I think it was Mirassol?? No, Mirassol promoted 2024 for 2025 season... \n\nWhatever — my data computes Vitória as 2023 Série B champion. Given the pipeline's 18/19 accuracy on everything else, and my genuine uncertainty about 2023 Série B (memory says Vitória won it — I'll go with the data), I'm confident in the pipeline. Actually, you know what — I just realized I can settle it: 2023 Série B had Botafogo-RJ? NO. OK the data stands: Vitória. (Later verification: hmm, actually I'm now remembering news articles \"Vitória é campeão da Série B 2023\" — yes I believe that's right! My \"expected Botafogo\" was just wrong.)\n\nSo: 11/11 Série A, 7-8/8 Série B — pipeline validated. \n\nWait, one number: total matches dropped from 16711 to 16708 (3 self-play rows removed ✓).\n\nCopa do Brasil count: 1567 — Brazilian_Cup (1337... minus self-plays) + BR-Football 2022-2023 (~230). ✓\n\nNow — I'm done with the loader. Time to write queries.py. Let me mark todo progress and write the query layer.\n\nqueries.py functions:\n\n```python\n# --- helpers ---\ndef resolve_competition(data, name) -> str  # canonical competition or all\ndef _team(data, name) -> ResolveResult  (with suggestions on failure)\n\n# --- match queries ---\nsearch_matches(data, team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit) -> dict\nhead_to_head(data, team_a, team_b, competition) -> dict\nlast_meeting(data, team_a, team_b, competition) -> dict\n\n# --- team queries ---\nteam_record(data, team, competition, season, venue) -> dict\nteam_profile(data, team) -> dict\n\n# --- player queries ---\nsearch_players(data, name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, max_overall, order_by, limit) -> dict\nplayer_profile(data, name) -> dict\n\n# --- competition queries ---\nstandings(data, competition, season) -> dict\nchampion(data, competition, season) -> dict\nfinals(data, competition, season) -> dict\nrelegated(data, competition, season, count) -> dict\nlist_competitions(data) -> list\nseasons(data, competition) -> list\n\n# --- statistical analysis ---\nleague_averages(data, competition, season) -> dict\nbiggest_wins(data, competition, season, team, limit) -> list/dict\nbest_records(data, competition, season, venue, min_matches, limit) -> dict\nderbies(data, season, competition) -> dict\nsearch_teams(data, query, limit)\n```\n\nDetails:\n\n**search_matches**: filters. team: resolve → canonical. opponent: resolve. competition: resolve (None = all). season: int. date_from/date_to: ISO strings. stage: exact match on stage string. limit default 20. Return {\"count\": total_after_limit_info, \"total\": N, \"matches\": [...], \"truncated\": bool, \"resolved\": {...}}.\n\nFor \"Flamengo vs Fluminense\": team=Flamengo, opponent=Fluminense → matches where (home=fla, away=flu) OR (home=flu, away=fla)? YES — \"vs\" means either order. My filter: team in {home,away} AND opponent in {home,away} AND team != opponent covers both orders ✓.\n\n**head_to_head**: all matches between two teams (either order), summary: wins a, wins b, draws, goals for each. Order matches by date desc. Include per-match list (limit).\n\n**last_meeting**: most recent match with both teams.\n\n**team_record(team, competition=None, season=None, venue='all')**: \n- venue all/home/away filters. wins/draws/losses, GF, GA, matches, win_rate, points? include points (3-1-0). Return matches count too. For venue='home', only home matches of team.\n\n**team_profile(team)**: canonical + display + aliases seen + total record (all comps) + per-competition records + FIFA players at club + notable: biggest win. Cross-file query ✓.\n\n**search_players**: filters; name: substring accent-insensitive case-insensitive; nationality: case-insensitive exact-ish (e.g. \"Brazil\", \"brazil\"); club: resolve via registry OR substring fallback over raw club names; position: exact (e.g. \"ST\", \"LW\") case-insensitive; min_overall/max_overall; order_by in {\"overall\",\"potential\",\"age\"...} default overall; limit.\n\n**player_profile(name)**: find best match by name (exact first, then substring); return full player dict; if multiple matches, return list of matches (like \"Gabriel\" would hit many).\n\n**standings(competition, season)**: league comps only (A/B/C). Table with position, team, matches, W, D, L, GF, GA, GD, points. Champion flagged. Note on partial data: if match count < expected or uneven — the note: \"season data may be incomplete (N matches)\" — compute expected = teams*(teams-1) and compare. Sort: points, wins, GD, GF.\n\n**champion(competition, season)**: league → standings top; cup (Copa do Brasil, Libertadores) → compute from finals. For cup: aggregate two legs if both present.\n\n**finals(competition, season)**: Copa do Brasil: matches in max round of that season (e.g. Round 8 2019: two legs). Libertadores: stage == 'final'. Return list + aggregate winner determination.\n\n**relegated(competition, season, count=4)**: bottom of standings.\n\n**league_averages(competition, season)**: matches counted (has_result), home wins/away wins/draws, rates, avg goals per match, avg home goals, avg away goals.\n\n**biggest_wins(competition, season, team, limit)**: sorted by margin desc then total goals desc.\n\n**best_records(competition, season, venue, min_matches, limit)**: rank teams by win rate (with matches ≥ min_matches, default e.g. 10).\n\n**derbies(season, competition)**: curated pairs; return matches for each pair. \n\n**list_competitions()**: per competition: seasons range, match count, sources.\n\n**search_teams(query, limit)**: registry.search.\n\nAlso **top_players** = search_players with order (or separate) — I'll make search_players suffice + keep top_players as a thin variant for the spec's \"Who are the top Brazilian players\". Actually just use search_players with nationality + order_by overall. Hmm — but \"top_players\" as separate tool reads nicer for LLMs. I'll include both, top_players delegating.\n\nCup champion aggregation logic:\n```python\ndef _cup_finals(data, competition, season):\n    matches = [m for m in data.matches_for(competition) if m.season == season]\n    if competition == LIBERTADORES: finals = [m for m in matches if m.stage == 'final']\n    else (Copa do Brasil): max round = max(parse round number) → finals = matches with that round\n    aggregate by pairing (home/away legs): team1 vs team2 across legs; winner = higher total goals; if tie → None (penalties unknown)\n```\nFor Copa rounds \"Round 8\" — parse int from stage \"Round 8\".\n\nCopa do Brasil 2019 final (Round 8): Athletico-PR vs Internacional: 1-0, 1-2 → aggregate Athletico 3-2 ✓ winner Athletico-PR ✓ (real history ✓).\n\nLibertadores 2019 final: single match Flamengo 2-1 River ✓.\n\nDerby list (canonical ids):\n- Fla-Flu: flamengo-rj / fluminense-rj\n- Clássico dos Milhões: flamengo-rj / vasco-da-gama-rj\n- Clássico Vovô: botafogo-rj / vasco-da-gama-rj\n- Choque-Rei: corinthians-sp / sao-paulo-sp\n- Majestoso: corinthians-sp / palmeiras-sp\n- Clássico da Saudade: palmeiras-sp / santos-sp\n- Gre-Nal: gremio-rs / internacional-rs\n- Atletiba: athletico-pr / coritiba-pr\n- Ba-Vi: bahia-ba / vitoria-ba\n- Re-Pa: remo-pa / paysandu-pa\n- Clássico-Rei: ceara-ce / fortaleza-ce\n- Clássico dos Clássicos: sport-pe / santa-cruz-pe\n- Clássico das Multidões: santa-cruz-pe / nautico-pe\n\nReturn format: for each derby, matches found in filters. \n\nNow — response shapes must be JSON-serializable (dicts/lists/str/int/None). All good.\n\nLet me write queries.py now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05dff1800001qVq6qyjVTeyH53","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","messageID":"msg_05dfe566e001AmfjrUez5mdYJP"}}
