olResult):\n        if inspected_return_ann.metadata:\n            return_type_expr = inspected_return_ann.metadata[0]\n            if len(inspected_return_ann.metadata) >= 2:\n                # Reconstruct the original annotation, by preserving the remaining metadata,\n                # i.e. from `Annotated[CallToolResult, ReturnType, Gt(1)]` to\n                # `Annotated[ReturnType, Gt(1)]`:\n                original_annotation = Annotated[\n                    (return_type_expr, *inspected_return_ann.metadata[1:])\n                ]  # pragma: no cover\n            else:\n                # We only had `Annotated[CallToolResult, ReturnType]`, treat the original annotation\n                # as being `ReturnType`:\n                original_annotation = return_type_expr\n        else:\n            return FuncMetadata(arg_model=arguments_model)\n    else:\n        original_annotation = effective_annotation\n\n    if structured_output is None and _returns_content(return_type_expr):\n        # Content blocks and the Image/Audio helpers are what the model reads, not data for the\n        # application: a derived schema would advertise the block's own model as output_schema (and,\n        # unless the tool builds its own CallToolResult, echo every block into structured_content).\n        # structured_output=True still forces one.\n        return FuncMetadata(arg_model=arguments_model)\n\n    output_model, wrap_output = _create_output_model(original_annotation, return_type_expr, func.__name__)\n\n    if output_model is not None:\n        try:\n            # FuncMetadata builds the validator and schema on construction, so an unsupported return type\n            # surfaces here, at registration, rather than on the first call.\n            return FuncMetadata(arg_model=arguments_model, output_model=output_model, wrap_output=wrap_output)\n        except (\n            PydanticUserError,\n            ForbiddenQualifier,\n            NameError,\n            TypeError,\n            ValueError,\n            pydantic_core.SchemaError,\n            pydantic_core.ValidationError,\n        ) as e:\n            # These are expected errors when a type can't be converted to a Pydantic schema\n            # PydanticUserError: When Pydantic can't handle the type (e.g. PydanticInvalidForJsonSchema);\n            #   subclasses TypeError on pydantic <2.13 and RuntimeError on pydantic >=2.13\n            # ForbiddenQualifier, NameError: an invalid qualifier or unresolvable annotation on a TypedDict key,\n            #   met while rebuilding a stdlib TypedDict below 3.12 (pydantic reports both as PydanticUserError)\n            # ValueError: When there are issues with the type definition (including our custom warnings);\n            #   arrives wrapped in a ValidationError when raised during FuncMetadata construction\n            # SchemaError: When Pydantic can't build a schema\n            # ValidationError: When validation fails\n            logger.info(f\"Cannot create schema for type {return_type_expr} in {func.__name__}: {type(e).__name__}: {e}\")\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"output_schema\\|output_model\\|wrap_output\" /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mcp/server/mcpserver/utilities/func_metadata.py | head -20 && sed -n 380,470p /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-809922ee1cb6/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/mcp/server/mcpserver/utilities/func_metadata.py","time":{"start":1788283757930,"end":1788283757932}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the SDK's `convert_result`: structured_content is produced when `output_model` is not None — i.e. when an output schema exists (derived from the return annotation). My functions annotate `-> dict`. For a bare `dict` annotation, the schema is `{\"type\": \"object\"}`... and then `adapter.validate_python(result)`... For plain `dict` annotation the output_model may be None (no schema derivable) → text-only.\n\nBut the FIRST call DID produce structured content with `-> dict`... hmm, actually did it? Let me recheck — the first call printed `sc.get('total')` successfully... Hmm wait, maybe I misread and the first call worked because... let me look again at the earlier successful output: `isError: False | total: 44 | first: ...` — hmm that was with `res.is_error`... wait no, that run failed at line 13 before printing... Actually NO — the first e2e run printed TOOLS and then failed on isError. The second run printed `isError: False | total: 44 | first: 2003-04-13 Flamengo` — wait no, that was the direct query smoke test. Let me re-read the second e2e run output... it failed with `res3.structured_content.get` NoneType. Hmm — but before that, line 11-12 printed... no, the traceback hid stdout? The run showed only the exception. Then in my isolated test of the error case: `structured: None` + text content.\n\nSo maybe the FIRST call ALSO returned structured None and `sc.get('total')` would have failed... In run 2, the print on line 11 (`isError: False | total: ...`) — I didn't see it in the output because the exception group traceback consumed it? The output showed nothing before the traceback. Hmm, stdout may have been buffered and lost... unclear. Let me just test directly: does `search_matches` with valid args produce structured_content?\n\nThe important thing: with `-> dict` annotations, results may come back as TEXT only (the dict gets JSON-dumped into a TextContent). Clients can still read it (res3.content[0].text contained the JSON). That's usable but NOT ideal — structured output is better for LLM tooling.\n\nHow to get structured content in mcp 2.x? The function's return annotation must produce an output schema. For rich structured output, the SDK wants a Pydantic model or TypedDict? Let me check how output_model is derived — func_metadata creation: `structured_output` param and output schema from type hints. Let me look at how the Tool derives output_schema for `-> dict`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e04a24a001YPeWAwlR9Ja9HC","sessionID":"ses_fa214f21cffeb5QAv8JGSE5SjQ","messageID":"msg_05e04608f001fH1y8r8SssC0MZ"}}
